    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is a WHO guideline?",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-11-what-is-a-who-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A WHO guideline is any document developed by the World Health Organization containing recommendations for clinical practice or public health \npolicy. A recommendation tells the intended end-user of the guideline what \nhe or she can or should do in specific situations to achieve the best health \noutcomes possible, individually or collectively. It offers a choice among different interventions or measures having an anticipated positive impact on \nhealth and implications for the use of resources.\nRecommendations help the user of the guideline to make informed decisions on whether to undertake spec",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a WHO guideline as any WHO document containing recommendations for clinical practice or public health policy. Explains that a recommendation tells the intended user what to do in specific situations to achieve the best health outcomes and helps users decide, select and prioritize among interventions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Why does WHO develop guidelines?",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-12-why-does-who-develop-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO develops guidelines whenever Member States, WHO country offices, \nexternal experts or other stakeholders ask for guidance on a clinical or public \nhealth problem or policy area. This generally happens when they are uncertain \nabout what to do or how to choose among a range of potential policies or interventions. Uncertainty can be triggered by a new public health problem or emergency; the uncovering of new evidence; an absence of good-quality evidence (or \nof any evidence at all); or a change in resource availability or access to services.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that WHO develops guidelines when Member States, country offices, experts or other stakeholders ask for guidance because they are unsure what to do. Lists the triggers of such uncertainty: a new problem or emergency, new evidence, a lack of good evidence, or a change in resources or access to services.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What principles underlie WHO guidelines?",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-13-what-principles-underlie-who-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The Twelfth General Programme of Work (GPW) of WHO (2014) specifies \nthat in its normative and standard-setting work, WHO is and will remain \na science- and evidence-based organization with a focus on public health. \nGuidelines are the fundamental means through which the Organization fulfils its technical leadership in health, as identified in the GPW. The GPW \nstates that “WHO’s legitimacy and technical authority lie in its rigorous \nadherence to the systematic use of evidence as the basis for all policies” (1).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the principle that WHO is a science- and evidence-based organization, citing its general programme of work. States that guidelines are the main means of exercising technical leadership and that WHO's authority rests on systematic use of evidence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1\n2\nWHO has adopted internationally recognized methods and standards \nfor guideline development to ensure that its guidelines are of the highest \nquality. WHO guidelines must be developed in observance of the following \nprinciples:\n■■\nGuidelines address an area of uncertainty and an unmet need for \nguidance.\n■■\nGuidelines reflect the core WHO value of the “right to health” (2).\n■■\nThe process of developing recommendations is explicit and transparent: the user can see how and why a recommendation was developed, \nby whom, and on what basis.\n■■\nThe process of developing guidelines is multidiscipl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the principles WHO guidelines must follow, after noting that WHO uses internationally recognized methods and standards. The principles include addressing a real area of uncertainty, reflecting the right to health, transparent and explicit development, multidisciplinary input, minimizing bias, systematic weighing of benefits and harms, public availability of evidence, adaptability to local settings, and tailoring to a specific audience.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the aim of this handbook?",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-14-what-is-the-aim-of-this-handbook.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This handbook presents the important principles of guideline development \nand provides stepwise instruction on the technical and procedural aspects \nof developing a WHO guideline. It aims to steer readers through the process to ensure that the resulting guideline is credible; the recommendations \naccurately reflect the balance of potential benefits and harms; the guideline \nmeets WHO’s publication standards in terms of content, methods and presentation; the underlying evidence is accessible; and the recommendations \nare implementable and positive in their impact on population health.\nChapter 1",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States the aim of the handbook: to set out principles and stepwise instruction for developing a WHO guideline. Says the goal is a credible guideline whose recommendations balance benefits and harms, meet WHO publication standards, rest on accessible evidence and can be implemented to benefit population health.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "For whom is this handbook intended?",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-15-for-whom-is-this-handbook-intended.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This handbook is intended for:\n■■\nany WHO department, programme or staff member wishing to produce a guideline;\n■■\nmembers of a WHO guideline steering group;\n■■\nmembers of a WHO guideline development group (GDG);\n■■\nmembers of a WHO guideline external review group; and\n■■\nanyone interested in understanding how WHO develops guidelines.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the intended readers of the handbook: WHO staff and departments wishing to produce a guideline, members of the steering, development and external review groups, and anyone wanting to understand how WHO develops guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is in this handbook?",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-16-what-is-in-this-handbook.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This handbook follows the entire development of a WHO guideline − from determining if a guideline is needed through to eventual publication. The guideline \ndevelopment process and its primary contributors are summarized in Table 1.1.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the handbook follows guideline development from deciding whether a guideline is needed through to publication, and points to a table summarizing the process and its main contributors.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Types of WHO guidelines",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-17-types-of-who-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "It is important to consider what type of guideline will best fit the intended \npurpose, as this will determine the methods, resources and time frame for \ndevelopment, finalization and dissemination (see Table 1.2). WHO guidelines comprise a broad spectrum of products that vary mainly in terms of \nthe following features:\n■■\npurpose;\n■■\nscope;\n■■\nthe point in time at which the guideline is being developed relative to \nthe life-span of an intervention;\n■■\nthe organizations or entities developing the guideline;\n■■\nthe presence in the guideline of new versus previously published recommendations; an",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the type of guideline determines methods, resources and time frame, and that types differ in purpose, scope, timing, developer, new versus existing recommendations and timeline. Names four main types (standard, consolidated, interim, and emergency or urgent-need) plus other less frequent types. Presents a table of the development process by stage, contributor, step and chapter, from planning through publishing and updating, and a table comparing each type's purpose, scope, developer, recommendations and development period.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Standard guidelines",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-171-standard-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A standard guideline covers a clinical or policy area (e.g. the treatment \nof postpartum haemorrhage or the minimum requirements for the safe \ndelivery of care to people with HIV infection). Such guidelines vary \ngreatly in scope and focus: they might address the use of a single drug \nfor a disease or condition, such as naloxone injection by lay persons for \nsuspected opioid overdose, or they might encompass the full scope of a \ncondition or public health problem, such as the diagnosis, screening and \ntreatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Recommendations in a standard \nguideline are either de",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes standard guidelines, which cover a clinical or policy area and can range from a single drug to the full scope of a condition, with recommendations either new or updated. Lists their usual focus areas such as clinical interventions, health-system policy, public health exposures, diagnostic tests and surveillance, and states they typically take 9 to 24 months, need systematic reviews and one or two group meetings, and have a review date. Notes that most WHO guidelines are of this type.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Consolidated guidelines",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-172-consolidated-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A consolidated guideline (also known as a compilation of guidelines) contains recommendations from existing WHO guidelines, or from guidelines \nproduced by other organizations that have followed processes consistent \nChapter 1 \n Introduction\n7\nwith those used by WHO (see Section 1.8.2). Producing consolidated guidelines is complex because existing guidelines may need to be updated and \nnew recommendations may have to be added to address important gaps in \nthe existing guidance. In addition, maintaining the document is difficult, \nsince individual recommendations may become outdated at differen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a consolidated guideline as a compilation of recommendations from existing WHO guidelines or from other organizations' guidelines produced by consistent processes. Explains that it is complex to produce and maintain, that all included recommendations must be up to date and approved by the review committee, and that cross-referencing is required. Sets out when committee review is needed and the one case in which it is not.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Interim guidelines",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-173-interim-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Interim guidelines are produced when WHO is asked to provide guidance when \nthe available data and information are most certainly incomplete, especially if \nadditional data are anticipated in the near future. This can occur when an intervention − a drug, a medical device or a health practice, for example − has just \nappeared or is about to be made available; a new disease or condition has emerged; \nor new data on an existing intervention, exposure, disease or condition have been \nreported. Interim guidelines usually have a very focused scope and a short shelf-\nlife. They should always clearly",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes interim guidelines, produced when guidance is requested while data are incomplete, for example for a new intervention, disease or data. Says they have a narrow scope and short shelf-life, must state when more evidence is expected, and still comply fully with the handbook's processes and standards despite pressure for speed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guidelines in response to an emergency or urgent need",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-174-guidelines-in-response-to-an-emergency-or-ur.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Certain circumstances preclude the development of a standard guideline. This is \nthe case when WHO must provide guidance in response to public health emergencies. Depending on the type of event or situation, such guidelines may need to \nbe produced within hours, days, weeks or months. Their purpose and the methods used to develop them vary with the time frame in which guidance is required.\nAlthough guidelines that must be produced rapidly cover a wide spectrum, it is convenient to consider two basic types:\n■■\nEmergency (rapid response) guidelines ‒ Public health emergencies \nmay necessitate a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes guidelines produced for emergencies or urgent need and distinguishes two basic kinds. Emergency (rapid response) guidelines may rely on existing guidelines or expert opinion within hours to days but must still keep basic quality principles and document their rationale. Rapid advice guidelines follow WHO processes more closely, carry a review-by date and need committee review at both stages. Also notes that urgent recommendations outside an emergency must rest on a systematic review and meet all handbook standards.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Additional types of guidelines produced by WHO",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-18-additional-types-of-guidelines-produced-by-wh.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "empty"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guidelines developed in collaboration with external organizations",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-181-guidelines-developed-in-collaboration-with-e.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "organizations\nClinical and public health guidelines are produced by many organizations, \nincluding national agencies, intergovernmental organizations and specialist \nmedical societies. From time to time it may be appropriate for WHO to col-\nlaborate with these groups to produce a joint guideline.\nCollaborative guidelines must adhere to the basic principles for WHO \nguidelines. When developing such guidelines, it is particularly important to \nnote that:\n■■\nno funding can come from commercial sources, and conflicts of interest must be declared and managed as per WHO procedures;\n■■\na systematic r",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how WHO may jointly produce guidelines with external organizations, which must still follow WHO's basic principles. Lists requirements including no commercial funding, managed conflicts of interest, a systematic review, transparent methods, representative experts and consultation on publishing. States that staff must submit a planning proposal highlighting any deviation, which the review committee assesses case by case.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guidelines developed by external organizations",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-182-guidelines-developed-by-external-organizatio.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Guidelines that serve WHO’s purpose may already exist and WHO may consider adopting, adapting or incorporating all or part of a guideline developed \nby an external organization. In order to adopt or endorse such a guideline, \nWHO staff in the relevant technical unit must carefully evaluate the guideline’s quality and applicability to WHO’s needs.\nThe guideline must meet WHO’s standards for guidelines as depicted \nin this handbook. The two standards that externally developed guidelines \ncan seldom meet are: (a) applicability to a global target audience (most notably low- and middle-income count",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers guidelines developed by external organizations that WHO might adopt, adapt or incorporate, which requires careful evaluation of quality and applicability. Says such guidelines rarely meet two standards, global applicability and expert panels representing all WHO regions, so adoption is rare. Specifies what the planning proposal for an endorsed or adapted guideline must contain.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Adaptation of existing WHO guidelines",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-183-adaptation-of-existing-who-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO guidelines originally intended for one setting may be adapted for use \nin another. For example, guidelines on routine obstetric care can be adapted \nfor emergency settings. Adaptations of WHO guidelines must follow standard GRC procedures, including GRC review of the planning proposal and the \nfinal guideline. Guidelines may also be adapted in emergency or urgent situations, when existing guidelines need to be quickly identified and assessed. \nThe GRC Secretariat is developing further guidance on when and how to \nadapt existing guidelines in such situations.\nChapter 1 \n Introduction\n11",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that WHO guidelines made for one setting may be adapted to another, for example routine obstetric care adapted for emergency settings. States that adaptations follow standard review-committee procedures and that further guidance on adapting in urgent situations is being developed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-018",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Information products that are not considered guidelines",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-018-19-information-products-that-are-not-considered.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "guidelines\nCertain types of documents are not considered guidelines and do not need \nto be submitted to the GRC for review. They include:\n■■\ndocuments that state established principles (e.g. human rights, WHO \nconstitutional issues);\n■■\nWHO Secretariat reports and other papers submitted to the Governing Bodies (Executive Board, Regional Committees, World Health \nAssembly);\n■■\ninformation documents that report facts, describe evidence, or document or review existing practices and interventions, provided that such \ndocuments are not making recommendations or advocating commitment of resources;\n■",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists document types that are not considered guidelines and need no committee review, such as statements of established principles, governing-body reports, purely informational documents, manufacturing standards, operational how-to manuals and standard operating procedures. Advises consulting the committee secretariat when unsure.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-019",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The Guideline Review Committee",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-110-the-guideline-review-committee.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The GRC was established by WHO’s Director-General in 2007 to ensure that \nWHO guidelines are of high quality, that they are developed using a transparent and explicit process, and that, to the extent possible, recommendations are based on evidence (3).\nThe GRC is composed of approximately 30 individuals, including representatives from all WHO regions as well as external members, and meets \nmonthly to review submitted documents.\n12",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the Guideline Review Committee, established by the Director-General in 2007 to ensure WHO guidelines are high quality, transparently developed and evidence-based. Says it has about 30 members from all WHO regions plus external members and meets monthly.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-020",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Terms of reference of the GRC",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-020-1101-terms-of-reference-of-the-grc.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "All WHO publications containing recommendations must be approved by \nthe GRC according to WHO policies and procedures (4). The GRC reviews \nevery WHO guideline twice during its development – once at the initial \nplanning stage and again after the recommendations have been developed \nand the guideline document has been finalized and edited.\nThe review of a planning proposal includes an assessment of whether \nthe proposed guideline development process is consistent with the steps \ndescribed in this handbook. The review of final submissions ensures that \nthe approved process has been followed and",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the committee's terms of reference: it must approve all WHO publications containing recommendations and reviews each guideline twice, at the planning stage and after the final document is edited. Explains what each review checks, that the committee advises on process and methods but not technical content, and how its approval fits into WHO's internal clearance sequence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-021",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The GRC Secretariat",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-021-1102-the-grc-secretariat.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The GRC Secretariat has several important roles. In addition to supporting \nthe GRC, it provides WHO staff with technical advice on guideline development, sets benchmarks, and evaluates guideline development processes, \ndocuments and standards to achieve quality assurance and improvement for \nWHO guidelines. More specifically, the GRC Secretariat:\n■■\ncoordinates and provides technical support on guideline development \nto WHO technical units at headquarters and in the regional offices;\n■■\norganizes training on guideline development methods and procedures for WHO staff;\n■■\nprovides administrativ",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the roles of the review committee secretariat, which supports the committee, advises WHO staff, sets benchmarks and evaluates guideline processes for quality assurance. Itemizes its tasks, including coordinating technical support, organizing training, collaborating with methodological networks and keeping a database of submissions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-022",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "References",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-022-111-references.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1.\t\nTwelfth General Programme of Work: not merely the absence of disease. Geneva: World \nHealth Organization; 2014 (http://www.who.int/about/resources_planning/twelfth-gpw/\nen, accessed 11 November 2014).\n2.\t\nConstitution of the World Health Organization. Geneva: World Health Organization; 1946 \n(http://apps.who.int/gb/bd/PDF/bd47/EN/constitution-en.pdf, accessed 11 November 2014).\n3.\t\nInformation Note - Establishment of a WHO Guidelines Review Committee [intranet site: \navailable to WHO staff only]. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2007 (http://intranet.\nwho.int/admin/infonotes/documents/16",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A numbered list of six references for Chapter 1, covering the general programme of work, the WHO Constitution, the committee's establishment note and WHO manual and intranet pages. The extraction also includes the opening sentence of Chapter 2 on the value of early planning.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-023",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is this guideline really needed?",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-023-21-is-this-guideline-really-needed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "When planning to develop a guideline, you must begin by asking a critical question: Is this guideline really needed? Guideline development is time-\nconsuming and expensive. You must carefully consider several issues before \nembarking on the process.\nPriority-setting in the normative work at WHO needs continuous evaluation and revision as priorities evolve within and across technical units. \nRequests for guidelines exceed the number of guidelines that can be produced with available resources. In keeping with the need to prioritize, be \nsure to ask certain key questions before deciding to develo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens the question of whether a guideline is really needed, noting that development is time-consuming and expensive and that requests exceed capacity. Advises asking key questions before deciding to proceed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-024",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who wants the guideline?",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-024-211-who-wants-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Have one or more WHO Member States requested it? WHO guidelines must \ngenerally meet global or regional needs and have a public health perspective.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks whether Member States have requested the guideline, noting that WHO guidelines should generally meet global or regional needs and take a public health perspective.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Should WHO be the organization to produce this guideline?",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-212-should-who-be-the-organization-to-produce-th.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Have WHO’s governing bodies requested the guideline? Is WHO in the best \nposition to issue guidance on this topic? Does the topic fall within the scope",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Poses questions on whether governing bodies requested the guideline and whether WHO is best placed to issue guidance on the topic; the text breaks off mid-sentence because of the page split.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Planning guidelines",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-2-planning-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "15\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n16\nline owing to its resources, implementation skills and experience, or its local \nand regional knowledge?\n2.1.3 Do guidelines on the topic already exist? \nHave guidelines on the same topic already been issued by other departments at WHO or by other organizations? Do they address the needs of \nWHO’s Member States? Avoid duplicating existing work by consulting \nthe relevant departments, the GRC Secretariat and the WHO library. \nIf existing guidelines are identified, examine their quality and applicability to the current need. Consider updating existing",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Mixes the end of the question on whether WHO should produce a guideline, which refers to its resources, implementation skills and local knowledge, with the section on whether guidelines on the topic already exist. Advises consulting WHO departments, the review committee secretariat and the library to avoid duplication, and examining or updating existing guidelines that are out of date.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What purpose and audience does the guideline serve?",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-027-22-what-purpose-and-audience-does-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO staff developing guidelines need to have a clear goal and audience in \nmind when they set about developing a guideline.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the section on purpose and audience, stating that staff need a clear goal and audience in mind when developing a guideline.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-028",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What will the guideline achieve?",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-221-what-will-the-guideline-achieve.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Will the guideline address poor practice or change programme approaches \nor health policy while aiming to fulfil the right to health? Will the guideline \nprovide direction for users in areas in which they feel uncertain about what \nto do? Resolving uncertainty should be the focus of most guidelines.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Poses questions about what a guideline will achieve, such as addressing poor practice or changing programmes or policy while fulfilling the right to health. States that resolving users' uncertainty should be the focus of most guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-029",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What are the specific objectives of the guideline?",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-029-222-what-are-the-specific-objectives-of-the-guid.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "What health indicators or other measures do you hope to improve? What test \nor approach do you wish to evaluate and provide guidance on? Clear, achievable objectives will govern and facilitate the development of your guideline.\nChapter 2 \nPlanning guidelines\n17",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks what health indicators, tests or approaches a guideline targets and says clear, achievable objectives will govern and facilitate its development.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-030",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who is the target audience?",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-030-223-who-is-the-target-audience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Guidelines must have a clearly defined target audience (end-user) which is \nidentified early in the guideline development process, and the recommendations need to be tailored to that audience. (Note that the end-users are those \nwho will apply the guidelines, not the recipients of the recommended interventions.) Writing documents to meet the needs of policy-makers, health-\nsector managers, clinicians and other health professionals simultaneously is \nnot straightforward and should be avoided.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that a guideline needs a clearly defined target audience identified early, with recommendations tailored to it. Clarifies that end-users apply the guideline while recipients receive the interventions, and warns against writing for policy-makers, managers and clinicians at once.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-031",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who are the recipients of the interventions?",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-031-224-who-are-the-recipients-of-the-interventions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Who are the service users and other individuals whose health and well-being will \nbe affected by the recommended interventions? Attention to their perspectives and \nneeds must be built into the guideline development process from the beginning.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks who the service users and other recipients of the recommended interventions are, and says their perspectives and needs must be built into guideline development from the beginning.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-032",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "When is the guideline needed?",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-032-23-when-is-the-guideline-needed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The optimal time to start developing a guideline and the time frame for completing the task must be carefully considered at the outset.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the best start time and the completion time frame for a guideline must be considered carefully at the outset.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-033",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Why now?",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-033-231-why-now.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Is this the best time to develop recommendations, or are new data expected \nto emerge in the near future? Will the existing infrastructure or policies \nchange in a manner likely to affect guideline implementation?\n2.3.2 When is the guideline needed? \nThe projected timeline for guideline development needs to be matched with \nthe time when the guidance is needed. Realistically, a high-quality standard \nguideline will take at least 9 to 12 months to produce if it has a fairly narrow \nscope. On the other hand, if the guideline aims to cover a large number of \nquestions and involves many de novo sy",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks whether now is the best time to develop recommendations, given expected new data or changes in infrastructure or policy that could affect implementation. Also says the timeline must match when guidance is needed, with a narrow high-quality standard guideline taking at least 9 to 12 months and a broad one with many new systematic reviews taking 2 to 3 years.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-034",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is the guideline a response to a situation calling for urgent advice?",
      "pageStart": 30,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-034-233-is-the-guideline-a-response-to-a-situation-c.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "advice? \nIf so, the guideline development process and methods may differ from those \nused for standard guidelines. Under a compressed timeline, the scope of \na guideline may have to be greatly reduced. In addition, rapid review and \nguideline development processes and methods can result in an end product \nwith a high risk of bias. WHO staff must balance this risk with the demand \nto produce recommendations rapidly (see Chapter 11).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that a guideline responding to urgent advice needs different processes and methods, often a greatly reduced scope. Warns that rapid methods carry a high risk of bias, which staff must balance against the demand for speed, and refers to Chapter 11.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-035",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Will the recommendations in the guideline be implemented?",
      "pageStart": 30,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-035-24-will-the-recommendations-in-the-guideline-be.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "implemented?\nThe purpose of WHO guidelines is to improve the health and well-being of \nindividuals and populations. To accomplish that, guidelines need to be disseminated, adopted or adapted, and their recommendations implemented.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that guidelines aim to improve health and therefore must be disseminated, adopted or adapted, and implemented. Introduces the question of whether recommendations will actually be implemented.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-036",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Are the guideline and its implementation within the relevant programme’s work area and budget?",
      "pageStart": 30,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-036-241-are-the-guideline-and-its-implementation-wit.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "programme’s work area and budget?\nUnder the WHO reform (1) all work at WHO should fall within the scope of the \nGeneral Programme of Work and Programme Budget, as agreed with WHO’s \nGoverning Bodies. If the guideline does not fall within this remit, it will be difficult to justify the resources and budget needed to complete the work.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that under WHO reform all work must fall within the General Programme of Work and Programme Budget, so a guideline outside that remit will be hard to justify in resources and budget.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-037",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is a process for disseminating and implementing the recommendations in place?",
      "pageStart": 30,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-037-242-is-a-process-for-disseminating-and-implement.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "recommendations in place?\nWho is likely to implement the recommendations? What level of guidance \nis needed and in what format? What infrastructure and services are needed \nfor implementation? Is access to the service an important consideration? \nThese issues should be considered from the start, as they affect who should \nbe involved, what resources are required and how the recommendations are \ncrafted and presented.\nChapter 2 \nPlanning guidelines\n19",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Poses questions about who will implement the recommendations, what level and format of guidance is needed, and what infrastructure, services and access are required. Says these issues should be considered from the start because they affect who is involved, resources and how recommendations are written.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-038",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who should be involved in developing a guideline?",
