      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-040",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What WHO departments must be involved?",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-040-252-what-who-departments-must-be-involved.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Do other departments at headquarters or in WHO’s regional offices, including those with cross-cutting mandates, have an interest in the guideline, \nhave relevant expertise or are producing similar products? Prepare a list \nof technical units at WHO and of key external organizations, experts and \nstakeholders that will need to be consulted or involved in the guideline \ndevelopment process.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks which other headquarters and regional departments, including those with cross-cutting mandates, have interest, expertise or similar products. Advises preparing a list of WHO technical units and external organizations, experts and stakeholders to consult.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-041",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Have you identified experts in guideline methods?",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-041-253-have-you-identified-experts-in-guideline-met.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Generally WHO staff commission systematic review and guideline development experts to assist in formulating the key questions; identifying, \nassessing and synthesizing the evidence; and translating the evidence into \nrecommendations. Such experts should be identified early in the guideline \ndevelopment process.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says WHO staff usually commission experts in systematic reviews and guideline development to help with key questions, evidence work and turning evidence into recommendations, and that they should be identified early.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-042",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Additional considerations",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-042-26-additional-considerations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Once you are certain that a guideline is needed by its intended end-users \nand will benefit the service users or other recipients of the recommended \nmeasures or interventions, you need to consider several other factors before \nembarking on the project.\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n20",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces further factors to consider once a guideline is judged needed by its end-users and beneficial to recipients, before starting the project.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-043",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Do you have the necessary resources to develop a guideline?",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-043-261-do-you-have-the-necessary-resources-to-devel.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "To produce a high-quality guideline, you will need resources to identify or \nperform systematic reviews, convene meetings and cover production costs. \nNote that WHO cannot develop guidelines using money from commercial \nentities (see Chapter 6). Guideline development also involves long hours of \ndedication by WHO staff.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks whether resources are sufficient for systematic reviews, meetings and production costs. Notes that WHO cannot use commercial money for guidelines and that development demands long hours from staff.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-044",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What publication types and formats are you considering?",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-044-262-what-publication-types-and-formats-are-you-c.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "What level of detail and format will your target audience find most useful? \nGuideline users are not generally interested in a detailed description of how \nguidelines are developed to conform to WHO standards, so you should plan \nand budget for derivative products such as summaries, algorithms or wall \ncharts specifically tailored for the end-users of the guideline. Electronic versions, perhaps accompanied by short paper publications, may be more practical and cheaper than print versions.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises considering the level of detail and format that users want, and budgeting for derivative products such as summaries, algorithms or wall charts, since users rarely want detail on methods. Suggests electronic versions with short paper publications may be cheaper and more practical.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-045",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What translations are you planning?",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-045-263-what-translations-are-you-planning.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "What languages are spoken by the people most in need of the advice in your \nguideline? Consider the implications for your budget and time frame and \ncarefully choose the language(s) for the translation of your guideline.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Asks which languages are spoken by those most in need of the advice and says to weigh translation implications for budget and timeline when choosing languages.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-046",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Scoping the guideline",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-046-27-scoping-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Scoping is the process of defining what the guideline will and will not include. \nTo establish the scope, you need to determine:\n■■\nthe areas of practice or policy to which the guideline applies;\n■■\nthe interventions, approaches or exposures of interest (i.e. the priority \ntopics);\nChapter 2 \nPlanning guidelines\n21\n■■\nthe individuals and/or populations (including subpopulations) that the \nrecommendations are intended to affect; and\n■■\nthe important outcomes − both benefits and harms – that may result.\nThe process of scoping a guideline will establish the focus for the recommendations, as well",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines scoping as deciding what a guideline will and will not include, and lists what must be determined: areas of practice or policy, interventions or exposures, affected populations and important benefit and harm outcomes. Explains that scoping sets the focus and key questions and keeps the guideline manageable, and calls it one of the hardest and most important steps because a wrong scope wastes resources and leaves users uncertain.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-047",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to scope the guideline",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-047-271-how-to-scope-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Scoping a guideline involves a series of steps, generally performed or coordinated by the responsible technical officer.\n■■\nDraft the potential scope ‒ Draft a proposed scope and a list of potential priority topics.\n■■\nSet up the WHO guideline steering group (“steering group” for the \nremainder of the text) ‒ Convene a small group of WHO staff, including representatives of all relevant departments, to provide feedback on \nthe proposed scope of the guideline.\n■■\nRefine the list of priority topics ‒ Identify the key issues: this determines the breadth and depth of the work. Do not try to include",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the steps of scoping, coordinated by the responsible technical officer: draft the scope, set up the steering group, refine priority topics, run a preliminary literature search, keep the topic list minimal, and formulate key questions in the population, intervention, comparator and outcome format. Stresses avoiding a textbook-style scope, focusing on areas of desired change, inequity, controversy or uncertainty, and considering equity, human rights and gender early.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-048",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The steering group",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-048-31-the-steering-group.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "scope, a summary of the scope should be circulated to the GDG and \nsometimes other stakeholders for comments. These groups should be \nreminded that WHO is producing a guideline, not a textbook, as the \nreviewers will almost invariably suggest expanding the planned scope.\n■■\nReconsider ‒ Once you have incorporated the external feedback as \nappropriate, review the scope and key questions again. Is the scope feasible? Is the time frame reasonable? Are sufficient financial and human \nresources available?",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Despite its heading, contains the closing steps of the scoping procedure: circulating a scope summary to the development group and other stakeholders while reminding them that a guideline is not a textbook, and then reconsidering feasibility, time frame and resources after feedback.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-049",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "References",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-049-28-references.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1.\t\nWHO reform [website]. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2014 (http://www.who.int/\nabout/who_reform/en, accessed 11 November 2014).\nWhen developing WHO guidelines, four groups need to be established \n(Table 3.1):\n■■\nthe steering group;\n■■\nthe GDG;\n■■\nthe external review group; and\n■■\nthe systematic review team.\nThese groups have different skills, perspectives, roles, responsibilities \nand tasks. They are established at different times, but all work to produce a \nhigh-quality guideline.\n3.1 The steering group\nOnce the technical unit has decided to proceed with developing a guideline, \nthe s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Begins with a single reference on WHO reform, then opens Chapter 3 by naming the four groups to be established: the steering group, the guideline development group, the external review group and the systematic review team. Describes the steering group's composition, size and time commitment, and lists its roles, from drafting the scope and managing conflicts of interest to organizing meetings, drafting the guideline, overseeing review and publication, and monitoring the need for updates.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-050",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The guideline development group",
      "pageStart": 36,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-050-32-the-guideline-development-group.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The GDG is made up of external experts whose central task is to develop evidence-based recommendations. The GDG also performs the important task \nof finalizing the scope and key questions of the guideline in PICO format. \nThis group should be established early in the guideline development process, \nonce the steering group has defined the guideline’s general scope and target \naudience and begun drafting the key questions.\nPotential members of the GDG are identified by the steering group and \nare selected to encompass the technical skills, diverse perspectives and geographic representation neede",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the guideline development group as external experts who develop evidence-based recommendations and finalize the scope and key questions. Explains when it is established, how members are chosen for skills, diversity and geography, the usual size of 10 to 20, and that it normally needs at least one face-to-face meeting.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-051",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Contributors and their role in guideline development",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-051-3-contributors-and-their-role-in-guideline-devel.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "25\nThe members of the GDG are not commissioned and do not receive any \nfinancial compensation other than for direct expenses associated with their \nwork on the guideline. The responsible technical officer may develop terms \nof reference so potential GDG members clearly understand their roles and \nresponsibilities before committing themselves. Members of the GDG participate in the guideline development process and at meetings as individuals \nand not as representatives of the institutions or organizations with which \nthey are affiliated.\nThe role of the GDG is to:\n■■\nprovide input into the scope",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Despite its heading, describes the development group's terms of participation and role: members are not paid beyond expenses and act as individuals rather than representatives of their institutions. Lists the group's roles, including giving input on scope, ranking outcomes, examining evidence profiles, interpreting benefits and harms, and formulating recommendations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-052",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Composition of the guideline development group",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/sec-052-321-composition-of-the-guideline-development-gro.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The GDG is multidisciplinary and composed of individuals from all WHO \nregions likely to use the guideline, except for employees of WHO or other \nUnited Nations organizations. Its membership should be balanced in terms \nof gender and geography. Possible conflict of interest is also an important \nconsideration when selecting and confirming GDG members (see Chapter 6). There are several ways to identify, nominate and select members of \nthe GDG. In addition to drawing members from established technical networks and WHO collaborating centres, consider publishing an open call for \nnominees. The aim",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how the development group should be composed: multidisciplinary, balanced by gender and geography, excluding WHO and UN staff, and screened for conflicts of interest. Describes member types and their value, including the chair and vice-chair or co-chairs, technical experts, end-users, representatives of affected people with the benefits of and barriers to service-user involvement, methods experts, an economist and an equity, human rights and gender expert.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-053",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The external review group",
      "pageStart": 40,
      "pageEnd": 41,
      "target": "../sections/sec-053-33-the-external-review-group.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The external review group is composed of persons interested in the subject of \nthe guideline as well as individuals who will be affected by the recommendations (often referred to as “stakeholders”). Thus, the external review group \nmay include technical experts, end-users, programme managers, advocacy \ngroups and individuals affected by the condition addressed in the guideline, \namong other stakeholders. This group is generally established by the steering group after the GDG is identified and once the guideline’s scope and \nChapter 3 \nContributors and their role in guideline development\n29\nkey",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the external review group of interested people and stakeholders, including experts, end-users, programme managers and affected individuals, and how and when the steering group establishes it. Says it should be balanced and may review the scope, key questions and the final guideline, where its role is to find errors, comment on clarity and implementation, and not to change recommendations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-054",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The systematic review team",
      "pageStart": 41,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-054-34-the-systematic-review-team.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Systematic reviews of the evidence are the basis for most types of recommendations (see Chapter 8). Because WHO staff usually lack the time to perform \nthese reviews, they normally commission them from external contractors. \nThese contractors should be identified very early in the guideline development process because they have expertise in the development of key questions and can help the steering group to establish a reasonable scope that \nconforms to the available budget and timeline.\nSystematic reviews can be commissioned from any group with the necessary expertise and no financial conflic",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that systematic reviews underpin most recommendations and are usually commissioned from external contractors with no financial conflicts, identified early, with the Cochrane and Campbell collaborations as possible sources. Then presents a multi-page table summarizing each contributing group, covering its responsibilities, functions, timing, establishment, leaders, expertise, size and whether declarations of interest are required, down to observers and funders.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-055",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Other individuals and groups involved in guideline development",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-055-35-other-individuals-and-groups-involved-in-guid.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "development",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A heading page for the section on other individuals and groups involved in guideline development, with no body text.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-056",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The guideline methodologist",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-056-351-the-guideline-methodologist.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "At least one methodologist – an expert in guideline development processes \nand methods − should be involved in the development of WHO guidelines. \nThe methodologist complements the technical expertise of the subject matter \nexperts, programme managers and other members of the GDG. The guideline \nmethodologist is an expert in systematic reviews, GRADE, and the translation \nof evidence into recommendations. They generally have experience formulating public health recommendations. Methodologists should be identified early \nin the guideline process so that they can participate in planning, scoping",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that at least one methodologist, an expert in guideline processes, systematic reviews, GRADE and turning evidence into recommendations, should be involved. Says they should be identified early to help with planning and key questions and should help the development group formulate recommendations transparently at meetings.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-057",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Consultants with additional technical expertise",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-057-352-consultants-with-additional-technical-expert.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Additional commissioned experts are sometimes involved in the development of a guideline. These individuals have expertise in other essential areas, \nsuch as decision analysis (modelling), economics or epidemiology. They \nmay play a variety of roles in the guideline development process and may be \ninvited to attend and present at GDG meetings. They do not contribute to the \nformulation of recommendations, however, unless they are official members \nof the GDG, in which case they would not have a contract with WHO (see \nSection 3.2).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes commissioned consultants with additional expertise such as modelling, economics or epidemiology, who may present at group meetings. States that they do not contribute to formulating recommendations unless they are official members of the development group.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-058",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Observers at the meeting of the guideline development group",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-058-353-observers-at-the-meeting-of-the-guideline-de.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "group\nStakeholders such as nongovernmental organizations, advocacy groups, \nfunders, target audiences, and service-users may be invited to send a member \nor representative to GDG meetings. Their role is to observe, although the \nmeeting chair may ask them for information or an opinion. They do not \nparticipate in the formulation of recommendations.\n34",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that stakeholders such as nongovernmental organizations, advocacy groups, funders and service users may be invited to send observers to development group meetings. Observers do not take part in formulating recommendations, though the chair may ask for their information.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-059",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Designated writer/editor",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-059-354-designated-writereditor.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "To ensure coherence, clarity and accuracy, one person should be respon-\nsible for drafting the guideline, incorporating comments from the GDG \nand peer review, and finalizing the document before publication. A clearly \nwritten guideline and a well-documented process are critical to the final \nclearance and usability of the guideline, so the writer needs to be involved \nin the planning and development stages, attend GDG meetings, and work \nclosely with the steering group and the GDG chair. The writer, who may be \na WHO staff member or can be commissioned, must be familiar with the \nWHO style gu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that one designated writer or editor should draft and finalize the guideline, incorporating group and peer-review comments. Says the writer should be involved from planning, attend meetings, know the WHO style guide and consult equity, human rights and gender experts on how people are described.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-060",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Funders of the guideline",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-060-36-funders-of-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The funder(s) of a guideline can play no role in the guideline development \nprocess and should not influence the recommendations. Private funders, \nincluding industry or foundations, may wish to observe GDG meetings, but \nthey must not contribute in any way to the systematic review and evidence \nappraisal process and, most particularly, to the formulation of recommen-\ndations. Governmental and intergovernmental agencies (e.g. the Centers \nfor Disease Control and Prevention [United States of America] or the Joint \nUnited Nations Programme on HIV/AIDS [UNAIDS]) may fund and/or \npartner in guidel",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that funders can play no role in development and must not influence recommendations. Private funders may observe meetings but not contribute to reviews or recommendations, while governmental and intergovernmental funders may partner, with their employees in the steering group but not the development group unless the dual role is declared and managed as a conflict of interest.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-061",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Identifying and managing conflicts of interest",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-061-37-identifying-and-managing-conflicts-of-interes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Chapter 6 and the website of the Office of Compliance, Risk Management and \nEthics (8) explain the steps followed to identify, manage and report conflicts \nof interest involving GDG members and commissioned experts. Declara-\nChapter 3 \nContributors and their role in guideline development\n35\ntions of interests (DOI) are collected and reviewed before making appointments to the GDG. Any subsequent changes in GDG members’ declaration \nof interests must be reported to the steering group. Potential candidates for \nmembership who have major conflicts of interest, be they financial or nonfinancial, ca",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Points to Chapter 6 and the compliance office for how conflicts of interest are identified and managed, and says declarations are collected before appointments and later changes reported to the steering group. States that major conflicts bar appointment while minor ones can be managed individually or at group level. Also begins the section on managing an effective group meeting, with the steering group and chair responsible.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-062",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The role of the chair and vice-chair",
      "pageStart": 47,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-062-381-the-role-of-the-chair-and-vice-chair.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "During meetings, the chair must ensure that GDG members can present their \nviewpoints and that all relevant issues are discussed in a respectful and efficient \nmanner. In addition, the chair should keep the group focused on the agenda; \nreflect on and summarize the opinions of GDG members; raise issues that \ncould inform the decision process; and manage the group so as to achieve consensus. The chair and vice-chair should not impose their own opinions on the \ngroup, however they may put forth their views in a purely personal capacity.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the chair's duties at meetings: letting members present views, keeping to the agenda, summarizing opinions, raising relevant issues and steering the group to consensus. Says the chair and vice-chair should not impose their opinions, though they may state them in a personal capacity.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-063",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Decision-making in the guideline development group",
      "pageStart": 47,
      "pageEnd": 48,
      "target": "../sections/sec-063-382-decision-making-in-the-guideline-development.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The steering group must formulate a plan delineating how decisions will \nbe made by the GDG. This plan must be formulated and agreed upon by \nthe group’s chair and vice-chair before the first GDG meeting. It should \nbe presented to GDG members at the beginning of this first meeting, with \nthe opportunity for questions and discussion. GDGs sponsored by WHO \nshould generally make their recommendations through consensus, which \ncan be achieved by various means. Acceptable approaches for group decision-making must be defined and made explicit before any recommenda\n36\ntions are formulated, and a pl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the steering group must plan how the development group will make decisions, agreed with the chair and vice-chair before the first meeting and presented there. States that groups should generally decide by consensus, that acceptable approaches and a fallback if consensus fails must be defined in advance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-064",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Planning an effective meeting",
      "pageStart": 48,
      "pageEnd": 48,
      "target": "../sections/sec-064-39-planning-an-effective-meeting.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Most guidelines involve at least one face-to-face meeting of the GDG to formulate recommendations. Some groups hold an initial meeting to finalize a \nguideline’s scope and key questions (see Chapter 7). GDG meetings generally \ncover a lot of material and in them a number of important decisions are made \nwithin a short time. The steering group must ensure that all in attendance \nunderstand their role and the expected outputs of the meeting by providing \nGDG members with clear information about how the meeting will be run \nand about the tasks and roles of those present.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that most guidelines need at least one face-to-face meeting of the development group, sometimes preceded by one to settle scope and key questions. Says the steering group must make sure attendees understand how the meeting runs, their roles and the expected outputs.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-065",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Goals of the meeting",
      "pageStart": 48,
      "pageEnd": 48,
      "target": "../sections/sec-065-391-goals-of-the-meeting.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The steering group should consider the following questions when planning \na meeting of the GDG:\n■■\nWhat is the purpose of the meeting?\n■■\nWhat are the specific objectives for the meeting?\n■■\nWhat decisions do not require deliberation by the GDG (and can \ntherefore be made by the steering group)?\n■■\nWhat specific decisions and outputs are expected from the \nmeeting?",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists questions the steering group should consider when planning a meeting, covering its purpose, specific objectives, decisions that need not involve the group, and the decisions and outputs expected.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-066",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Preparing for the meeting",
      "pageStart": 48,
      "pageEnd": 49,
      "target": "../sections/sec-066-392-preparing-for-the-meeting.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "■■\nHow are meeting participants expected to prepare before the meeting?\n■■\nWhat materials need to be sent to GDG members ahead of time and \nwhen should they be sent to allow adequate time for review?\n■■\nHave all DOI forms been collected and assessed and have appropriate \nmanagement plans been agreed upon with the director of the responsible technical officer’s technical unit?\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n37",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists questions on preparing for a meeting: how participants should prepare, what materials to send and when, and whether declaration of interest forms have been collected, assessed and given management plans.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-067",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Roles and process at the meeting",
      "pageStart": 49,
      "pageEnd": 49,
      "target": "../sections/sec-067-393-roles-and-process-at-the-meeting.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "■■\nWhat roles are played by the steering group, GDG members, the methodologist, the systematic review team’s representative and observers?\n■■\nHow will declarations of interest and conflicts of interest be presented? \nWhat will be the level of detail? How will new disclosures be assessed \nand managed at the GDG meeting?\nIf the purpose of the GDG meeting is to finalize the scope and the key questions for guideline development:\n■■\nWho will draft the scope and key questions and how will these questions be identified?\n■■\nWill GDG members have the opportunity to review the questions \nbefore the meet",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists questions about roles and process at the meeting, such as the roles of each participant type, how conflicts of interest are presented and managed, and recording of deliberations. Gives separate question sets for meetings that finalize the scope and key questions and for meetings that develop recommendations, covering evidence summaries, evidence profiles, draft recommendations and templates.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-068",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Follow-up after the meeting",
      "pageStart": 49,
      "pageEnd": 50,
      "target": "../sections/sec-068-394-follow-up-after-the-meeting.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "■■\nWhat follow-up will take place with meeting participants?\n■■\nIf recommendations are not finalized at the GDG meeting, how will \nthey be finalized after the meeting?\nChapter 3 \nContributors and their role in guideline development\n38",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists two questions on follow-up after the meeting: what follow-up occurs with participants, and how recommendations will be finalized if not completed at the meeting.",
        "state": "draft",
