      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What if there are major changes in scope or approach after the GRC has approved the planning proposal?",
      "pageStart": 55,
      "pageEnd": 56,
      "target": "../sections/sec-076-46-what-if-there-are-major-changes-in-scope-or-a.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "after the GRC has approved the planning proposal?\nIt is not unusual for changes to occur after the planning proposal is developed and approved by the GRC. New or unanticipated evidence, or new ideas \nfrom the steering group, the GDG or other interested parties, can engender \ndiscussions and the decision to change course. Such changes in planning \nproposals generally fall into one of the following categories:\n■■\nA major increase or decrease in scope ‒ Example: new key questions \nmight be added or, less commonly, questions might be removed.\n■■\nA change in the packaging of the guideline ‒ Example",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how to handle changes after the review committee approves the planning proposal, and lists the categories: major change in scope, change in packaging, major method change, change to a key question, and minor process changes. Says the first three kinds must be reviewed by the secretariat and sometimes the committee, while minor ones need not be, and the text includes the remainder of the proposal topics table covering writing, peer review, logistics, implementation and updating.",
