    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-084",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Social determinants of health and health inequities",
      "pageStart": 59,
      "pageEnd": 62,
      "target": "../sections/sec-084-523-social-determinants-of-health-and-health-ine.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The social determinants of health are the conditions in which people grow, \nlive, work and age (6). Health inequities are differences in health that are \nunfair, avoidable and remediable because they depend largely on the social \ndeterminants of health, which are amenable to improvement. WHO’s normative work, including guideline development, must thus take these determinants into consideration (7).\n5.2.4 Gender\n“Gender” refers to the roles, behaviours, activities and attributes that a given \nsociety considers appropriate for men and women and boys and girls. Thus, \ngender is socially construct",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines the social determinants of health and health inequities, and distinguishes gender from sex and describes how gender norms affect risk, access and outcomes. Explains how social determinants produce inequity and can be reduced through programmes and intersectoral action. Also reproduces a box of eight entry points for integrating equity, human rights and gender, from analysis of evidence on inequities to ensuring gender balance and accountability mechanisms in the guideline process.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-085",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Eight entry points for integrating equity, human rights, gender and the social determinants of health into WHO guidelines",
      "pageStart": 62,
      "pageEnd": 62,
      "target": "../sections/sec-085-53-eight-entry-points-for-integrating-equity-hum.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "rights, gender and the social determinants of health \ninto WHO guidelines\nWHO’s normative work, including its recommendations and guidelines, \nmust integrate equity, human rights, gender and the social determinants of \nhealth. Eight entry points (Box 5.1) provide a practical approach for integrating these aspects into WHO guidelines. The relevance of specific strategies \nvaries across guideline topic areas, but all strategies should be systematically \nconsidered in the guideline development process.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says WHO's guidelines must integrate equity, human rights, gender and social determinants and introduces the eight entry points as a practical approach, to be systematically considered although relevance varies by topic.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-086",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Incorporating equity, human rights, gender and social determinants into each step in guideline development",
      "pageStart": 62,
      "pageEnd": 62,
      "target": "../sections/sec-086-54-incorporating-equity-human-rights-gender-and.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "determinants into each step in guideline development\nEquity, human rights, gender and the social determinants of health must \nbe considered during all three phases of guideline development (Table 1.1) \n– planning, development, and publishing and updating – and during most \nsteps within each phase.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that equity, human rights, gender and social determinants must be considered in all three phases of guideline development (planning, development, and publishing and updating) and in most steps within each.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-087",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Phase 1: planning",
      "pageStart": 62,
      "pageEnd": 64,
      "target": "../sections/sec-087-541-phase-1-planning.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "5.4.1.1 Request for guidance on a topic (Chapter 1)\nIn their request for guidance, Member States or other stakeholders might \nexplicitly ask that equity, human rights, gender and the social determinants \nof health be addressed during guideline development. Whether or not this \nrequest is made explicit, examining any health issue through the lens of \nequity, human rights, gender and the influence of social determinants is \nimportant. Doing so will help to better understand the needs and gaps to be \naddressed and may lead to interventions that are more effective in the longer \nterm and that will",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how to integrate equity, human rights, gender and social determinants into the planning phase. Covers the request for guidance, planning that aims to level up health across groups with stratifiers such as PROGRESS-Plus, composition of the contributing groups, and the planning proposal and scoping. Explains that the population, intervention, comparator and outcome elements of key questions should each take account of social position and the right to health.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-088",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Phase 2: development",
      "pageStart": 64,
      "pageEnd": 65,
      "target": "../sections/sec-088-542-phase-2-development.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "5.4.2.1 Evidence retrieval and synthesis (Chapter 8)\nExisting systematic reviews seldom take equity, human rights, gender and \nsocial determinants into account, and a new review or an update of an existing review may be needed to analyse information about equity. Fortunately, \nthe volume of primary research in this field is increasing and the methods and tools for systematic reviews of evidence on equity and for combining qualitative and quantitative research findings are rapidly evolving. \nLooking beyond averages and disaggregating data sometimes calls for an \nadjustment of traditional data c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how to integrate these considerations into the development phase. Covers evidence retrieval and synthesis, including equity-focused reviews and tools, assessment of evidence quality on subpopulations where data are sparse, and formulating recommendations, where likely equity gains may support strong recommendations and sparse equity evidence may support conditional ones with a research agenda and monitoring.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-089",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Phase 3: publishing and updating",
      "pageStart": 65,
      "pageEnd": 66,
      "target": "../sections/sec-089-543-phase-3-publishing-and-updating.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "5.4.3.1 Producing and publishing the guideline (Chapter 12)\nThe final guideline and in its derivative products must convey clear messages with respect to equity, human rights, gender and social determinants. \nWHO handbook for guideline development\n54\nFor example, the process of developing a gender-sensitive guideline starts \nbefore the first words are put on paper and only ends when the last illustration has been chosen. Throughout the process careful attention should be \npaid to the use of language, the selection of case examples, the expressions \nused to refer to population groups, the choic",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes integration in the publishing and updating phase, saying the final guideline and derivative products must give clear messages on equity, human rights, gender and social determinants. Advises attention to language, examples, photographs and population descriptions from the start, and gender-sensitive, non-stigmatizing language in translations. The text ends at the heading for adaptation, implementation and evaluation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-090",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "References",
      "pageStart": 66,
      "pageEnd": 69,
      "target": "../sections/sec-090-55-references.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "determinants of health, gender equality and relevant international human \nrights standards and principles, should be included in the sections on implementation, monitoring and evaluation. The interventions recommended \nin a guideline pose the risk of increasing rather than reducing inequities, \ndepending on how they are implemented. For example, marginalized population groups might be ignored or strategies that make them the last to benefit might be used. Important gender differences might not be considered \nor unintended effects on certain subpopulations or population strata might \noccur. Suc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Completes the guidance on adaptation, implementation and evaluation, warning that implementation can increase inequities and that WHO staff must monitor effects on equity, human rights and gender. Then lists the Chapter 5 references and opens Chapter 6, which defines the basic concepts and principles of declaring and managing interests and how staff collect declarations and manage conflicts.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
