    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-091",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is a conflict of interest?",
      "pageStart": 69,
      "pageEnd": 70,
      "target": "../sections/sec-091-61-what-is-a-conflict-of-interest.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A conflict of interest is an important potential source of bias and diminished \ncredibility in the development of WHO guidelines. “A conflict of interest is a \nset of circumstances that creates a risk that professional judgment or actions \nregarding a primary interest will be unduly influenced by a secondary interest” (1). The Declaration of Interests form at WHO (2) contains a similar \ndefinition: [A conflict of interest is] “any interest declared by an expert that \nmay affect or reasonably be perceived to affect the expert’s objectivity and \nindependence in providing advice to WHO.”\nAs noted",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a conflict of interest as a set of circumstances creating a risk that professional judgment about a primary interest is unduly influenced by a secondary interest, and gives WHO's declaration-form wording. Explains the terms primary interest, secondary interest and undue influence, and argues that a conflict exists whether or not it affects decisions, so phrases such as potential or perceived conflict should be avoided.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-092",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What types of conflicts of interest exist?",
      "pageStart": 70,
      "pageEnd": 71,
      "target": "../sections/sec-092-62-what-types-of-conflicts-of-interest-exist.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Conflicts of interest are of two basic types: financial and nonfinancial. Both \nare relevant to guideline development. Financial interests can be directly \nmeasured in monetary units, such as for stocks or patents owned, money \nreceived for commissioned work, or an honorarium for a speaking engagement. Nonfinancial interests, on the other hand, cannot readily be measured \nin monetary units and are less tangible and thus more difficult to identify, \nmeasure and manage. They include any interest that could be reasonably \nperceived to affect an individual’s objectivity and independence while work",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Distinguishes financial conflicts, which can be measured in money such as stocks, patents, commissioned work or honoraria, from nonfinancial ones, which are harder to identify and include professional advancement, prestige or drive to publish. Notes that intellectual conflicts of interest are one nonfinancial type.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-093",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Declaration and management of interests",
      "pageStart": 71,
      "pageEnd": 71,
      "target": "../sections/sec-093-6-declaration-and-management-of-interests.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "59\nThe declaration of one or more secondary interests (declaration of \ninterests) does not automatically mean that a conflict of interest is present: there is a distinction between the two. A transparent and objective \nprocess for assessing a declaration of interests is required to determine if a \nconflict exists and what its effects might be, and to manage any significant \nconflict of interest. This chapter describes the processes and procedures \nfor identifying and managing conflicts of interest during guideline development at WHO.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that declaring an interest does not automatically mean a conflict exists, and that a transparent and objective assessment is needed to decide whether one exists and manage it. Introduces the processes for identifying and managing conflicts during WHO guideline development.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-094",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Why is a conflict of interest important in guideline development at WHO?",
      "pageStart": 71,
      "pageEnd": 71,
      "target": "../sections/sec-094-63-why-is-a-conflict-of-interest-important-in-gu.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "development at WHO?\nCertain data point to an association between the secondary interests of \nauthors and funders and study outcomes (4), and between conflicts of interest and the conclusions drawn from systematic reviews (5). Data on the effects \nof industry relationships and the intellectual interests of guideline sponsors \nand authors are confined to case studies (6). Conflicts of interest can introduce bias into almost every step of the guideline development process, from \nscoping and the development of key questions, to the formulation and presentation of recommendations. In addition, conf",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains why conflicts matter, citing associations between secondary interests and study outcomes and conclusions, with evidence on guideline sponsors limited to case studies. States that conflicts can bias almost every step and undermine credibility of recommendations, guidelines and WHO, so developers must handle them in a proactive, transparent and defensible manner.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
