    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-095",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What are WHO’s policies on conflicts of interest?",
      "pageStart": 71,
      "pageEnd": 72,
      "target": "../sections/sec-095-64-what-are-whos-policies-on-conflicts-of-intere.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Guideline developers at WHO must be familiar with WHO’s policies on \nobtaining declarations of interests and assessing and managing conflicts of \ninterest. An overview of the process for identifying, managing and reporting such conflicts during guideline development at WHO is presented in \nBox 6.1, with further details provided below. Various websites provide additional information about WHO policies and procedures related to conflicts \nof interest and guideline development (2, 7, 8). Additional information can \nbe obtained from the Office of Compliance, Risk Management and Ethics.\n60\nWHO hand",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says guideline developers must know WHO's policies on obtaining declarations of interests and assessing and managing conflicts, points to an overview box and further web resources, and names the compliance, risk management and ethics office as a source of information.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-096",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who should complete a declaration of interests form?",
      "pageStart": 72,
      "pageEnd": 75,
      "target": "../sections/sec-096-65-who-should-complete-a-declaration-of-interest.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "According to WHO rules and regulations, a declaration of interests form and an \nanalysis of all declarations must be performed whenever an individual provides \nindependent advice to WHO (7) − that is, whenever an expert is asked to serve \nin an advisory role in a personal capacity. In guideline development, this means \nthat anyone invited to participate in the development of a WHO guideline must \ncomplete the declaration of interests form and agree to the publication of a \nsummary of that declaration in the guideline (see Table 6.1 and Fig. 6.1). This \nincludes all members of the GDG, individu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains who must complete a declaration of interests form: anyone providing independent advice in a personal capacity, including development group members, systematic reviewers, methodologists and the writer, who must also agree to publication of a summary. States that observers representing organizations need not complete one but their affiliations must be transparent. Presents a five-step overview of identifying, managing and reporting interests and a table of declaration and management by group, plus a figure of the assessment flow whose content is only yes and no labels.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-097",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What interests need to be disclosed?",
      "pageStart": 75,
      "pageEnd": 76,
      "target": "../sections/sec-097-66-what-interests-need-to-be-disclosed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Relevant financial as well as nonfinancial interests should be disclosed and \nsubsequently assessed and managed in order to minimize bias in guideline \ndevelopment and to ensure credible recommendations. A financial conflict of \ninterest arises when an individual or organization receives income or mon-\netary support that is related to, or could be affected by, the outcome of the \nWHO meeting or activity in which they are involved. This includes both per-\nsonal financial interests and the interests of the individual’s immediate family \nmembers (defined as the spouse, or partner with whom one ha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines financial and nonfinancial interests that must be disclosed in guideline development and explains why they are assessed and managed. It lists examples of financial interests, such as paid work, research support, patents, grants and shares, notes the US$ 5000 reporting threshold (US$ 1000 for non-monetary research support), and gives examples of nonfinancial interests like prior publications, public positions and affiliations with advocacy organizations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-098",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Collecting declarations of interests",
      "pageStart": 76,
      "pageEnd": 76,
      "target": "../sections/sec-098-67-collecting-declarations-of-interests.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Declarations of interests are collected using the standard WHO form for \nexperts. Such a form should be completed, signed by the expert or potential \ncontractor, and reviewed and assessed before firm invitations to participate in \na guideline development process are issued or contracts are signed. External \nexperts and contractors should be instructed to notify the responsible tech-\nnical officer of any change in relevant interests, and declaration of interests \nforms should be updated on a regular basis. Declaration of interests forms \nare confidential documents that should be securely stored",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how declarations of interests are collected using the standard WHO form, which must be completed, signed and assessed before invitations or contracts are issued. It adds that experts must report changes, that forms are confidential and securely stored, and that a curriculum vitae is requested alongside the form.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-099",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assessing declarations of interests",
      "pageStart": 76,
      "pageEnd": 79,
      "target": "../sections/sec-099-68-assessing-declarations-of-interests.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Little is known about the effect of declarations and conflicts of interest on \nsomeone’s behaviour, how group participants and GDG members and chairs \nweigh or discount information or opinions presented by conflicted individu-\nals, and how guideline end-users interpret information on conflicts of inter-\nest. The following guidance is based on: the scant existing evidence; standard \nand best practices among leading guideline development groups interna-\ntionally; sound legal and ethical principles; and expert opinion. In practical \nterms, three basic steps are involved in assessing declarations",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the three steps for assessing declarations of interest: staff review of the form with the expert's CV, a decision on whether a conflict of interest actually exists, and a judgement of how severe it is. It notes how little evidence exists on the effects of conflicts and that the guidance rests on best practice and expert opinion. It includes Table 6.2, with principles for collecting, assessing and managing declarations (report all funders, no private sector role, avoid and minimize conflicts, no financial conflicts for chairs), and Table 6.3, with criteria for judging severity such as the value and scope of the interest, the individual's discretion and the possible harm.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-100",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Managing conflicts of interest at the individual level",
      "pageStart": 79,
      "pageEnd": 79,
      "target": "../sections/sec-100-69-managing-conflicts-of-interest-at-the-individ.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "All declarations of interests should be reviewed and assessed by the respon-\nsible technical officer in consultation with the director of the technical \nunit. These individuals must decide whether a conflict of interest is severe \nenough to require a management strategy. In other words, they must decide \nto what extent the individual can participate in development of the guide-\nline. Decisions are made on a case-by-case basis depending on the severity \nof the conflict of interest. Merely disclosing the conflict does not constitute \nan adequate management strategy: careful consideration must be",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that each declaration is reviewed by the responsible technical officer with the director of the technical unit, who decide case by case whether a conflict needs a management strategy. It stresses that disclosure alone is not adequate management.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-101",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guideline development group members",
      "pageStart": 79,
      "pageEnd": 81,
      "target": "../sections/sec-101-691-guideline-development-group-members.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "At the level of the individual potential GDG member, management options \nrepresent a spectrum that includes:\n■■\nNo action ‒ the conflict of interest requires no action beyond \ndeclaration at the GDG meeting and reporting in the published \nguideline.\n68\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n■■\nParticipation is restricted ‒ the individual is excluded from parts of \nmeetings and of the guideline development process.\n––\nThe conflicted individual can be excluded from the formulation of specific recommendations but allowed to participate in all \ndiscussions.\n––\nThe conflicted individual can be barr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the options for managing a guideline development group member's conflict of interest, from no action, through restricted participation, to full exclusion. It lists conflicts that preclude participation, such as holding over US$ 5000 in shares or a patent on a product under consideration, and situations where participation should be limited, such as recent industry employment or authorship of key studies. It also states that no evidence supports a particular monetary threshold, so processes must be explicit, transparent and defensible.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-102",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Peer reviewers",
      "pageStart": 81,
      "pageEnd": 82,
      "target": "../sections/sec-102-692-peer-reviewers.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Declarations of interests should be collected from all peer reviewers. When \nreviewers are affiliated with organizations that clearly have a vested interest in the outcome of specific recommendations, a declaration of interests is \nnot usually required; reporting the reviewer’s affiliation or institution in the \nguideline is considered sufficient disclosure.\nThe external review group, including peer reviewers, may have significant conflicts of interest. In fact, these individuals may have been selected \n70\nWHO handbook for guideline development\nbecause of their perspectives and secondary inter",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that declarations of interests are collected from all peer reviewers, although reviewers whose organizations clearly have a vested interest need only report their affiliation. It notes that reviewers with conflicts can still help anticipate controversy, but their declarations must be transparent and their comments assessed case by case.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-103",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Managing conflicts of interest at the group level",
      "pageStart": 82,
      "pageEnd": 82,
      "target": "../sections/sec-103-610-managing-conflicts-of-interest-at-the-group.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "While financial conflicts of interest are usually managed at the individual \nlevel, nonfinancial conflicts of interest (including intellectual ones) may be \nmanaged at both the individual and group level. Group composition is influential in any decision-making process and observational evidence shows a \ncorrelation between the composition of GDGs and specific recommendations. For example, physicians tend to recommend procedures that they \npersonally deliver, whereas multidisciplinary groups tend to be more conservative in their recommendations (11, 12). If individuals with a particular \nviewpo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that nonfinancial conflicts, including intellectual ones, may be managed at the group as well as the individual level because group composition influences recommendations. It cites evidence that physicians favour procedures they deliver and warns of committee stacking.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-104",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Minimizing the risk posed by conflicts of interest when constituting guideline development groups",
      "pageStart": 82,
      "pageEnd": 84,
      "target": "../sections/sec-104-6101-minimizing-the-risk-posed-by-conflicts-of-i.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "constituting guideline development groups\nThere is scant evidence upon which to base recommendations on the optimal composition of GDGs to minimize the risk that intellectual conflicts of \ninterest will bias their decisions. The following suggestions are based largely \nChapter 6 \nDeclaration and management of interests\n71\non expert opinion and standards recommended by other organizations (1). \n(See also the guidance in Chapter 3.)\n■■\nGDG chair, co-chairs and vice-chair\n––\nThe chair, co-chair or vice-chair of a GDG should not have any \nfinancial conflicts of interest.\n––\nIf the co-chairs or the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives suggestions for constituting guideline development groups to limit the risk of intellectual conflicts. It covers chairs without financial conflicts (possibly a methodologist), a majority of members with no conflicts, balancing perspectives, separating the GDG from the evidence review and writing tasks, commissioning methodologists, and including varied stakeholders.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-105",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Minimizing the risk posed by conflicts of interest during the guideline development process",
      "pageStart": 84,
      "pageEnd": 86,
      "target": "../sections/sec-105-6102-minimizing-the-risk-posed-by-conflicts-of-i.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "guideline development process\nAppropriate management of disclosures and conflicts of interest does not \nend with the assessment of the declarations and the formulation of a plan for \nthe management of conflicts. The responsible technical officer and the steering group have additional and continuous responsibilities.\n■■\nAlthough GDG members participate in guideline development at \nWHO in an individual capacity, they must all provide their institutional affiliations, along with the goals and purpose of their institutions at the beginning of each GDG meeting.\n■■\nThe declarations of interests made",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes ongoing responsibilities for managing conflicts during guideline development, including declaring institutional affiliations and presenting and updating declarations at each meeting. It then explains how declarations must be reported in the guideline (tabular or narrative, required for GRC approval), gives three worked examples of such reports, and states that forms are kept confidential for at least 10 years.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-106",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What sources of funding are appropriate for WHO guidelines?",
      "pageStart": 86,
      "pageEnd": 86,
      "target": "../sections/sec-106-612-what-sources-of-funding-are-appropriate-for.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "guidelines?\nWHO guidelines cannot be funded, in whole or in part, by private sector \nentities or by non-state actors that are not at “arm’s length” of a private sector \nentity (2). Private sector entities are commercial enterprises. That is to say, \nthey are businesses that are intended to make a profit for their owners. The \nterm also refers to entities that represent private sector entities, or are governed or controlled by private entities. This includes (but is not limited to) \nbusiness associations representing commercial enterprises; entities not at an \narm’s length of their commercial s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that WHO guidelines cannot be funded wholly or partly by private sector entities or non-state actors not at arm's length from them, and explains what counts as a private sector entity. It directs staff to make reasonable efforts to learn a funder's revenue sources and to consult the Office of Compliance, Risk Management and Ethics with questions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-107",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "References",
      "pageStart": 86,
      "pageEnd": 89,
      "target": "../sections/sec-107-613-references.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1.\t\nGraham RMM, Wolman DM, Greenfield S, Steinberg E, editors. Clinical practice guidelines \nwe can trust. Washington (DC): The National Academies Press; 2011 (http://www.nap.edu/\nopenbook.php?record_id=13058, accessed 11 November 2014).\n2.\t\nDeclaration of Interests (WHO Experts) [intranet site: available to WHO staff only]. Geneva: \nWorld Health Organization; 2014 (http://intranet.who.int/homes/cre/ethics/doiexperts/, \naccessed 11 November 2014).\n3.\t\nGuyatt G, Akl EA, Hirsh J, Kearon C, Crowther M, Gutterman D, et al. The vexing problem of \nguidelines and conflict of interest: a potential sol",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A reference list for the chapter on declaration and management of interests, citing books, journal articles on conflict of interest and industry sponsorship, and WHO intranet documents.",
