    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-108",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Types of questions",
      "pageStart": 89,
      "pageEnd": 89,
      "target": "../sections/sec-108-71-types-of-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "There are two basic types of questions: background and foreground questions. Background questions provide context and rationale for the guideline; \nforeground questions directly inform and underpin recommendations.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the two basic types of questions, background and foreground, stating that background questions give context and rationale while foreground questions directly inform recommendations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-109",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background questions",
      "pageStart": 89,
      "pageEnd": 90,
      "target": "../sections/sec-109-711-background-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "These questions pertain to important background information on the issues \nunder consideration and their context. They do not relate to evidence of the \ntype that directly informs recommendations, but they do lead to information \nwhich helps to frame and formulate the foreground questions. Background \nquestions may pertain, for instance, to definitions; the prevalence, burden \nand distribution of the problem or disease at hand; or the pathophysiologic \nmechanisms underlying the effects of exposures or potential interventions. \nHere are some examples:\n■■\nWhat are the potential mechanisms by whi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines background questions as those that supply context, such as definitions, disease prevalence and burden, or underlying mechanisms, and help frame the foreground questions. It gives examples, explains that they can usually be answered from a single cited valid source rather than a systematic review, and notes the exception where a prevalence question is central and becomes a foreground question.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-110",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Foreground or key questions",
      "pageStart": 90,
      "pageEnd": 90,
      "target": "../sections/sec-110-712-foreground-or-key-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "These questions form the basis of the search for the evidence that will underpin the recommendations. They will be of various types, depending on the \nnature of the guideline and the specific topics it will cover. When a potential \nrecommendation focuses on an intervention, the key questions pertain to \nthe efficacy, effectiveness and potential harms of the intervention, as well as \nto factors such as acceptability, feasibility, cost and cost–effectiveness, and to \nthe values and preferences of the people who will be affected by the recommendations. When the topic focuses on a diagnostic test,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes foreground or key questions as the basis of the evidence search behind recommendations, and explains how their focus varies by topic: efficacy, harms, acceptability, feasibility and cost for interventions; sensitivity and specificity for diagnostic tests; and baseline risk or relative risk for exposures. It illustrates each with example questions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
