    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-112",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formulating questions and selecting outcomes",
      "pageStart": 91,
      "pageEnd": 91,
      "target": "../sections/sec-112-7-formulating-questions-and-selecting-outcomes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "79\n■■\nIn multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis patients, does the addition \nof bedaquiline to WHO-recommended second-line drug therapy \nimprove outcomes, as reflected by culture conversion and the time to \nsuch conversion?\nForeground questions are the most important ones for guideline development. The evidence identified on these questions will be used by the GDG \nto underpin the recommendations and these questions usually require a systematic review and assessment of the quality of the evidence for selected outcomes using the GRADE approach (see Chapter 9) (1). Foreground questions \nshould theref",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Extraction put text under the wrong heading: this section ends the examples of foreground questions with a bedaquiline example for multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis. It then states that foreground questions are the most important for guideline development, usually need a systematic review with GRADE quality assessment, and should be framed in PICO format.",
