    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-116",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Comparator",
      "pageStart": 93,
      "pageEnd": 94,
      "target": "../sections/sec-116-723-comparator.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "What courses of action or exposures stand as alternatives with which to \ncompare those recommended in the guideline?\n■■\nThe comparator in a recommendation may be standard practice – \nincluding no specific treatment – or another intervention relevant to \ncurrent practice or policy.\n■■\nComparators may be placebo, no intervention, the standard of \ncare, a gold or reference standard diagnostic test, variations of the \nintervention under consideration (such as different dosages), a different level of exposure or intervention, or a completely different \nintervention.\n■■\nComparisons may also be estab",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists guiding questions for the comparator element of PICO, covering alternatives such as standard practice, placebo, no intervention and other interventions, and stresses that comparators close to current practice are most useful. It also begins the outcomes element, asking which benefits, harms and equity effects matter most to affected people and calling for input from experts, programme managers and those affected.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-117",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Examples of key questions in PICO format",
      "pageStart": 94,
      "pageEnd": 95,
      "target": "../sections/sec-117-725-examples-of-key-questions-in-pico-format.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following are well-formulated key questions:\n■■\nIn a rural population in a low-income country (Population), does \npaying higher salaries to health workers (Intervention), compared with \npaying standard salaries (Comparator), increase the number of health \nworkers in rural areas (Outcome) within a 5-year period?\n■■\nIn babies born to HIV-positive women (P), does screening with a new \nrapid diagnostic test (I), compared with standard diagnostic methods (C), accurately detect disease (O) by 12 months of age?\n■■\nIn an urban population (P), is exposure to an environmental chemical (I), compared",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents six well-formulated key questions in PICO format, with the population, intervention, comparator and outcome labelled in each. It then contrasts one poorly formulated question about a drug for multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis, lists what it leaves unspecified, and gives a better rewording.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-118",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Types of key questions",
      "pageStart": 95,
      "pageEnd": 98,
      "target": "../sections/sec-118-73-types-of-key-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Although the PICO framework was originally devised to address the efficacy, \neffectiveness and potential harms of an intervention, it can be applied to \nother types of questions, as outlined in Table 7.1. The general types of questions most relevant to WHO guidelines relate to the following:\n■■\nintervention efficacy and effectiveness;\n■■\nintervention harms;\n■■\ndiagnostic approaches and test characteristics;\n■■\nthe values and preferences of the individuals affected by an intervention;\n■■\nrisk assessment, including baseline risk (prognosis), and additional risk \nfrom a given exposure; and\nWHO ha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that PICO can be applied beyond intervention efficacy to the main question types relevant to WHO guidelines: efficacy and effectiveness, harms, diagnosis, values and preferences, and risk assessment. It introduces Table 7.1, which gives the syntax and an example for each type.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-119",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Broadly- versus narrowly-focused key questions",
      "pageStart": 98,
      "pageEnd": 99,
      "target": "../sections/sec-119-74-broadly--versus-narrowly-focused-key-question.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Questions based on the PICO format may be broad or narrow in scope. A \nbroad question will lead to a comprehensive summary of a larger body of evidence and more generalizable findings, but it may also require significantly \nmore resources to answer. A broad question may also yield very heterogeneous evidence, making interpretation difficult. On the other hand, a narrow \nquestion may be easier to manage, but the evidence might be sparse and the \nfindings less generalizable. Depending on the scope of the guideline and the \navailability of information, the steering group may decide to split a bro",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Compares broad and narrow PICO questions, noting that broad ones give generalizable but resource-heavy and heterogeneous evidence while narrow ones are manageable but sparser, with the steering group able to split broad questions. It gives paired examples and shows, with a type 2 diabetes screening example, how several key questions can inform one recommendation question.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-120",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Number of key questions",
      "pageStart": 99,
      "pageEnd": 99,
      "target": "../sections/sec-120-75-number-of-key-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The number of key questions for a guideline varies greatly, depending on the \nscope of the guideline (the number of areas of uncertainty) and the available \nresources and timeline. In addition, key questions vary greatly in scope and \ncomplexity for each component of PICO. As noted above, for some types \nof recommendations, several key questions are needed to provide the evidence base; for others, only one key question and one systematic review are \nrequired. There is no point in wrapping several key questions into one: the \nworkload and resources required remain the same.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that the number of key questions depends on guideline scope, resources and timeline, and that some recommendations need several key questions while others need one. It advises against combining several key questions into one because the workload stays the same.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-121",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Selecting and rating outcomes",
      "pageStart": 99,
      "pageEnd": 101,
      "target": "../sections/sec-121-76-selecting-and-rating-outcomes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The purpose of any recommendation is to achieve a net benefit. Thus, selecting the most important outcomes is critical to producing a useful guideline. \nThe value attached to a given outcome by different populations and subpopulations varies. For this reason, it is essential to ask members of the GDG \n− which should contain end-users, implementers, patient representatives, \npolicy-makers, technical experts and perhaps also other stakeholders – to \nidentify the key outcomes that need to be considered in a guideline.\nThe steering group should compose an initial list of relevant outcomes, \ninclud",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to select and rate outcomes: the steering group drafts a list of desirable, undesirable and equity-related outcomes, the GDG adds to it, and members rate each on a 1 to 9 scale (7 to 9 critical, 4 to 6 important, 1 to 3 unimportant) from the perspective of those affected. It describes using the mean or median score, in-person discussion or a Delphi approach, and limiting the list to about seven important or critical outcomes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-122",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Finalizing the key questions and priority outcomes",
      "pageStart": 101,
      "pageEnd": 101,
      "target": "../sections/sec-122-77-finalizing-the-key-questions-and-priority-out.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Key questions should be finalized by the steering group after receiving input \nfrom all the relevant experts, including end-users (e.g. programme managers, partner agencies, and consumer and patient groups). Because the number \nof questions that call for systematic reviews will be a major determinant of \nthe time and resources needed to complete the guideline, the steering group \nshould aim to restrict the questions to those dealing with the areas of greatest controversy and uncertainty. This will help to ensure a comprehensive \nand useful guideline. The following steps should be followed to d",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the steering group finalizes the key questions after input from experts and end-users, restricting systematic-review questions to areas of greatest controversy and uncertainty because they drive time and resources. It introduces the stepwise process shown in Figure 7.2.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
