      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Analytic frameworks",
      "pageStart": 103,
      "pageEnd": 104,
      "target": "../sections/sec-130-78-analytic-frameworks.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A diagram illustrating the components of the intervention or exposure, and \nits relationship to the population, comparator and outcomes, is an extremely \nuseful tool for the systematic review team, the steering group and the GDG. \nSuch diagrams go by different names: analytic frameworks, evidence models, \nlogic models, causal pathways, decision trees, etc. The analytic framework \nillustrates the various components of the key question, the relationships \namong these components and the underlying mechanisms and pathways by \nwhich interventions or other exposures affect health and other outcomes",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains analytic frameworks, which are diagrams showing how an intervention or exposure relates to the population, comparator and outcomes, and the pathways linking intermediate to distal health outcomes. It says they make evidence types and assumptions explicit and help explain GDG agreement and disagreement, and advises the steering group to develop them early, with input from the GDG, review team and methodologist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
