      "target": "../sections/sec-132-81-what-is-a-systematic-review.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A systematic review is “a review of a clearly formulated question that uses \nsystematic and explicit methods to identify, select, and critically appraise \nrelevant research, and to extract and analyse data from the studies that are \nincluded in the review” (1). If conducted properly, systematic reviews reduce \nthe risk of bias and improve the reliability and accuracy of conclusions based \non evidence. Systematic reviews should underpin all WHO recommendations on the efficacy, effectiveness, and harms of interventions; the use of \ndiagnostic tests; exposure limits or safety thresholds for vario",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a systematic review and explains that systematic reviews should underpin all WHO recommendations because they reduce bias and improve reliability. It lists seven characteristics of a systematic review, such as focused questions, explicit methods, predefined eligibility criteria, a comprehensive search and risk-of-bias assessment, and begins to note that non-systematic reviews lack these.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
