      "target": "../sections/sec-133-8-evidence-retrieval-and-synthesis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "93\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n94\nand low-quality systematic reviews should not inform WHO guidelines and \nWHO staff must be able to recognize these and understand their limitations.\nA systematic review is different from a meta-analysis. The latter term \nrefers to the quantitative synthesis (pooling) of an outcome across comparable \nstudies to achieve a pooled estimate of effect (see Section 8.3.5). A systematic \nreview may contain one or more meta-analyses, but this is not a requirement. \nLikewise, a meta-analysis may be a pooled estimate of an outcome from a \ncohort of selected st",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues from the previous section, stating that non-systematic and low-quality systematic reviews should not inform WHO guidelines and that staff must be able to recognize them. It distinguishes a systematic review from a meta-analysis, a quantitative pooling of outcomes, and says pooling studies not found through a systematic review should generally be avoided.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
