      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The search for existing systematic reviews in bibliographic databases, such \nas Medline and EMBASE, should be comprehensive and conducted on the \nbasis of the predefined key question(s). This search can be performed by the \nsteering group or can be commissioned to an external team. Search filters \nspecific for reviews are available in search engines such as PubMed or OVID \nand are useful for preliminary scoping. The characteristics of these search \nfilters vary, however. Furthermore, the filters cannot reliably distinguish \nbetween a systematic review and a non-systematic review or identify hi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes where to find existing systematic reviews: comprehensive searches of databases such as Medline and EMBASE, with the limits of review-specific search filters, and searches of the Cochrane Library for reviews and protocols. It also covers how the GRC Secretariat can link staff to Cochrane, and names DARE, Epistemonikos and Health Systems Evidence as other sources.",
