      "target": "../sections/sec-140-step-2-identify-information-sources-and-search-f.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "articles\nThe development and finalization of the strategies for searching for evidence \nthat meets the established inclusion criteria is a fundamental step in developing guidelines. It must be done correctly or the quality of the guideline may \nbe compromised. Engaging an expert in information science with specific \nexpertise in bibliographic database searching related to systematic reviews is \nessential. Several individuals, including topic experts and one or more systematic review and guideline methodologists, should review the draft search \nstrategies before they are finalized. The systemat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes step 2, designing and running the search for studies, which needs an information science expert, review of draft strategies and consultation with a WHO information scientist. It explains the trade-off between search sensitivity and precision and the need to search for studies from low- and middle-income countries, including regional databases under Global Index Medicus.",
