      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Step 4: assess risk of bias of the individual studies",
      "pageStart": 113,
      "pageEnd": 114,
      "target": "../sections/sec-142-step-4-assess-risk-of-bias-of-the-individual-stu.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Each study included in a systematic review should be assessed for risk of bias. \nThe main types of bias for intervention studies include selection, attrition, \nperformance and detection bias (22). Nonrandomized studies have similar \nsources of bias, the most important being selection bias, which results in \nthe presence of systematic differences in the baseline characteristics of the \ngroups being compared. Chapter 9 provides additional details on how to \nassess the risk of bias at the individual study level.\nThe following resources may be helpful when determining which instrument to use to as",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes step 4: assessing the risk of bias of each included study, naming the main bias types (selection, attrition, performance and detection) and pointing to Chapter 9 for detail. It lists four instruments or resources, including Cochrane risk of bias tools and reports from Canadian and United States agencies.",
        "state": "draft",
