      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Step 5: synthesize and report the results and conclusions",
      "pageStart": 114,
      "pageEnd": 115,
      "target": "../sections/sec-143-step-5-synthesize-and-report-the-results-and-con.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The findings of the systematic review may be synthesized in a narrative \nmanner or quantitatively with a pooled estimate of effect for certain outcomes \n(in other words, a meta-analysis). The review should describe how data were \nhandled and why a given approach to synthesis was taken for each outcome.\nThe results of the systematic reviews are presented to the GDG, along \nwith an assessment of the confidence in the estimates of effect for the critical \nand important outcomes (i.e. quality assessment using GRADE [see Chapter 9]). This presentation generally occurs at an in-person meeting during",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes step 5, synthesizing and reporting results, either as a meta-analysis when studies are sufficiently homogeneous or as a narrative synthesis when they are not. It explains that results go to the GDG with a GRADE confidence assessment, that meta-analyses give pooled estimates shown in forest plots, and that narrative methods and subgroup analyses should be specified in advance.",
        "state": "draft",
