      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Step 6: prepare the final report of the systematic review",
      "pageStart": 115,
      "pageEnd": 115,
      "target": "../sections/sec-144-step-6-prepare-the-final-report-of-the-systemati.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Systematic reviews conducted in developing WHO guidelines should be \nreported in a standard format using the PRISMA reporting guidelines (20). \nFor some purposes, the methods and results section of the review may be \nall that WHO will require; for other purposes it may require a full report \nwith detailed introduction and discussion sections. GRADE evidence profiles should generally be part of the final report of the systematic review (see \nChapter 9). This report should be sufficiently detailed for WHO staff and the \nGDG to know exactly what was done and what the findings are. However, \nexces",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes step 6: reporting the review in the standard PRISMA format, with GRADE evidence profiles generally included. It says the level of detail may vary by purpose, but the report must make clear what was done and found without excessive study-level detail.",
