      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Including other types of data in the systematic review",
      "pageStart": 116,
      "pageEnd": 116,
      "target": "../sections/sec-146-85-including-other-types-of-data-in-the-systemat.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Many other types and sources of data may be relevant to WHO guidelines. \nFor example, for questions about substances that are potentially toxic to \nhumans or the environment, mechanistic and animal studies may be relevant. Questions about cost and measures of economic efficiency, such as cost–\neffectiveness, may require information on prices from manufacturers or data \nfrom programme evaluations, or may involve modelling based on evidence-\ninformed assumptions. The values and preferences of the people affected by a \nguideline may be explored by conducting a literature search or by generating",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists other data types that may be relevant to WHO guidelines, including mechanistic and animal studies for toxic substances, price and programme data or modelling for cost questions, surveys for values and preferences, and data on feasibility, equity and infrastructure.",
