      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Updating existing systematic reviews",
      "pageStart": 116,
      "pageEnd": 117,
      "target": "../sections/sec-147-86-updating-existing-systematic-reviews.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Updating an existing systematic review can be a less expensive and more efficient \nway to obtain the synthesis of the evidence required to underpin a recommendation. Updating a systematic review is a complex process, however. Like all the \nmethods outlined in this chapter, it calls for significant experience and training \nif it is to be done well. Once a high-quality and applicable systematic review is \nidentified, its authors should be contacted by the responsible technical officer to \nsee if they are planning to update the review or are doing so already. This is particularly relevant for Coc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses updating an existing systematic review as a cheaper route to the needed evidence that is nonetheless complex and demands experience. It advises contacting the original authors, outlines difficulties such as missing information, outdated methods and unavailable data, and concludes that updating is reasonable when a high-quality, fully reported review using current methods exists.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-148",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to commission a systematic review",
      "pageStart": 117,
      "pageEnd": 118,
      "target": "../sections/sec-148-87-how-to-commission-a-systematic-review.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A new systematic review is needed if a relevant, high-quality systematic \nreview is not identified. Or if a high-quality review is not up to date, it will \nneed to be updated. If WHO lacks the appropriate expertise and resources \n(i.e. staff time) for these tasks, then it will have to commission them from an \nexternal source.\nPerforming systematic reviews correctly takes time, expertise and \nresources and the systematic review team needs to be carefully selected. \nMembers of the Cochrane Collaboration may be commissioned to update an \nexisting Cochrane review or to perform a review de novo. Th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains when and how to commission a systematic review, covering when a new review or update is needed, how cost depends on expertise, question complexity, literature volume and languages, and the use of Cochrane members. It lists five steps for the steering group, from a request for proposals to quality assessment, and requires commissioned teams to know GRADE and produce evidence profiles, with detailed terms of reference.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
