    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-153",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the “quality of the evidence”?",
      "pageStart": 122,
      "pageEnd": 122,
      "target": "../sections/sec-153-91-what-is-the-quality-of-the-evidence.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Once the evidence has been retrieved and synthesized through a well conducted systematic review, its quality needs to be assessed. In the context of \nevidence syntheses, the GRADE working group defines the quality of the \nevidence as the “extent to which one can be confident that an estimate of \nthe effect or association is correct” (6). In the context of guideline development, the quality of the evidence reflects the confidence that the estimates of \nan effect are adequate to support a particular decision or recommendation. \nAlternative terms to describe the quality of the evidence are “certa",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines the quality of the evidence, in GRADE terms, as the extent to which one can be confident that an effect estimate is correct, and in guideline terms as confidence that estimates are adequate to support a recommendation. It gives the alternative terms certainty of the evidence and confidence in the estimates of effect.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-154",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What are GRADE evidence profiles?",
      "pageStart": 122,
      "pageEnd": 122,
      "target": "../sections/sec-154-92-what-are-grade-evidence-profiles.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "GRADE evidence profiles contain the assessment of the quality of the evidence and a summary of findings across studies for each important or \ncritical outcome and each key question (in PICO format). The GDG uses \nthese summaries as the basis for its discussions and to formulate recommendations (Table  9.1) Outcomes are listed in rows and the judgements \nmade about the factors that determine the quality of the body of evidence \nare described briefly for each outcome, along with a summary of the effect \nestimates for each. Additional details are provided in explanatory footnotes. \nFurther exampl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that GRADE evidence profiles hold the quality assessment and a summary of findings for each important or critical outcome and key question. It says the GDG uses them as the basis for discussion, with outcomes in rows, brief judgements on quality factors and explanatory footnotes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-155",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who performs the quality assessment?",
      "pageStart": 122,
      "pageEnd": 124,
      "target": "../sections/sec-155-93-who-performs-the-quality-assessment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Assessing the evidence and developing evidence summaries are specialized \ntasks that should be performed by a methodological expert with experience \nusing GRADE. Systematic review teams commissioned to support guideline \ndevelopment at WHO must have expertise in using GRADE to conduct evidence assessments.\nChapter 9 \nEvidence assessment\n111\nTable 9.1.\t\nExample of a GRADE evidence profile: is cryotherapy preferable to no treatment in women with \nhistologically confirmed cervical intraepithelial neoplasia? \n  \nCI: confidence interval; CIN: cervical intraepithelial neoplasia; GRADE: Grading of Re",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that evidence assessment is a specialized task for a methodological expert experienced with GRADE, and that commissioned review teams must have this expertise. The extraction also includes Table 9.1, a GRADE evidence profile example on cryotherapy for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, with its footnotes and very low quality ratings.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-156",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the role of WHO staff in assessing the quality of the evidence?",
      "pageStart": 124,
      "pageEnd": 124,
      "target": "../sections/sec-156-94-what-is-the-role-of-who-staff-in-assessing-th.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "of the evidence?\nWHO staff responsible for developing guidelines need to understand the \nbasic concepts governing the assessment of the quality of the evidence − and \nhow to apply them in GRADE evidence profiles − to supervise commissioned \nwork and advise GDG members. WHO staff who perform systematic reviews \nin house must have an in-depth knowledge of GRADE.\nGDG members should be briefed on how to assess the quality of the evidence before meeting to formulate recommendations. Depending on their \nfamiliarity with guideline development and GRADE, this can be done with a \ncombination of the onl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the role of WHO staff in quality assessment: they must understand GRADE well enough to supervise commissioned work and advise the GDG, and in-house reviewers need in-depth knowledge. It also says GDG members should be briefed on evidence assessment before meetings, using online modules, publications or an introduction by the methodologist or GRC Secretariat.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-157",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How is the quality of the body of evidence assessed for intervention studies?",
      "pageStart": 124,
      "pageEnd": 125,
      "target": "../sections/sec-157-95-how-is-the-quality-of-the-body-of-evidence-as.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "for intervention studies?\nGRADE categorizes the quality of the evidence as high, moderate, low or \nvery low (Table 9.2). These quality ratings apply to the body of evidence for \neach outcome assessed for each key question and not to individual studies. A \njudgement on the risk of bias of each individual study included in the body \nof evidence is needed, however, to assess the quality domain of study limitations (see Section 9.5.1.1).\nFor key questions that address interventions (6–8), the starting point \nor baseline for rating the quality of the evidence is always the study design, \nbroadly cl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that GRADE rates the quality of a body of evidence for each outcome as high, moderate, low or very low, not individual studies. It states that for intervention questions the starting point is study design, randomized trials versus nonrandomized and observational studies, and advises presenting different designs separately rather than pooling them in a meta-analysis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-158",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Five factors can lower the quality of the body of evidence for each outcome",
      "pageStart": 125,
      "pageEnd": 131,
      "target": "../sections/sec-158-951-five-factors-can-lower-the-quality-of-the-bo.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "each outcome\nA body of evidence based on RCTs is rated as being of high quality at the \noutset; evidence from nonrandomized trials or observational studies is \nrated as being of low quality. For both types of studies, these initial ratings can be adjusted in light of five factors: limitations in study design and \nexecution; indirectness; imprecision; inconsistency; and publication bias \n(see Fig. 9.1). For a given body of evidence, the ratings are conducted for \neach outcome. This requires detailed knowledge of the individual studies \nincluded in the body of evidence. The factor “limitations i",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that randomized trial evidence starts as high quality and observational evidence as low, and that five factors can lower the rating: study limitations, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision and publication bias. It includes Table 9.2 defining the four quality levels, advises recording reasons for downgrading in footnotes without rating mechanically, and details the criteria for each factor, such as randomization and blinding for trials and confounding control for observational studies. It also covers how imprecision is judged by sample size and how publication bias is suspected and handled.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
