    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-159",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Three factors can increase the quality of the evidence from observational studies",
      "pageStart": 131,
      "pageEnd": 132,
      "target": "../sections/sec-159-952-three-factors-can-increase-the-quality-of-th.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "observational studies\nObservational studies are considered low quality evidence from the outset by virtue \nof their design. If, and only if, there are no further limitations, such as additional \nrisk of bias (if, in other words, there is no reason to downgrade their quality), then \nthe assessor can consider upgrading the quality of the evidence according to three \ncriteria: dose−response gradient; direction of plausible bias; and the magnitude of \nthe effect. (It is important to note that factors that could reduce the quality of the \nevidence are always considered before those that could serve",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that observational evidence, low quality from the start, can be upgraded only if there is no reason to downgrade it, using three criteria: a dose-response gradient, a direction of plausible bias that would understate the effect, and a large magnitude of effect. It describes how precision and features such as rapid or consistent effects bear on upgrading, and says upgrades can be by one or two levels.",
        "state": "draft",
