    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-161",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is GRADE applicable in all situations?",
      "pageStart": 133,
      "pageEnd": 133,
      "target": "../sections/sec-161-97-is-grade-applicable-in-all-situations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Confusion and concerns exist as to whether GRADE is applicable to all situations in which a GDG issues a recommendation regarding an intervention. \nThe strength of the GRADE approach rests on the use of a structured framework for the assessment of the quality of the evidence and on the requirements that processes be explicit and judgements transparent. GRADE has \nbeen applied to a wide range of interventions in clinical medicine, public \nhealth and health policy  (18). While the ease of applying the GRADE \napproach will vary according to the type of evidence being assessed, the \ncircumstances",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Considers whether GRADE applies in all situations, concluding that its structured, transparent framework has been used widely and that cases where it cannot be applied are rare and need a rationale approved by the GRC. It says GRADE also covers diagnostic tests, prognosis and baseline risk, while methods for environmental exposures, economic efficiency, network meta-analyses and modelling are still under development.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
