      "target": "../sections/sec-166-1022-values-and-preferences.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This describes the relative importance assigned to health outcomes by those affected by \nthem; how such importance varies within and across populations; and whether this importance or variability is surrounded by uncertainty. The less uncertainty or variability there is \nabout the values and preferences of people experiencing the critical or important outcomes, \nthe greater the likelihood of a strong recommendation.\nBalance of benefits and harms\nThis requires an evaluation of the absolute effects of both benefits and harms (or downsides) \nof the intervention and their importance. The greater t",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the remaining rows of Table 10.1, describing how each factor affects the likelihood of a strong recommendation: values and preferences, balance of benefits and harms, resource implications, priority of the problem, equity and human rights, acceptability and feasibility. The text under the heading is the table body, which says that greater certainty, net benefit or advantage on each factor makes a strong recommendation more likely.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-167",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Detailed criteria that should be considered when moving from evidence to recommendations",
      "pageStart": 137,
      "pageEnd": 138,
      "target": "../sections/sec-167-102-detailed-criteria-that-should-be-considered.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "moving from evidence to recommendations\n10.2.1 Quality of the evidence\nThe quality of the evidence – the degree of confidence in the estimates of \neffect – is a key factor in determining the strength of a recommendation \n(see Chapter 9). The higher the quality of the evidence, the more likely a \nstrong recommendation is warranted. Uncertainty can surround both benefits and harms and if the corresponding evidence associated with one or \nmore important or critical outcomes is of low or very low quality, a conditional recommendation is much more likely.\n10.2.2 Values and preferences\nThe values an",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives detailed guidance on the first two criteria for moving from evidence to recommendations. It explains that higher quality evidence favours a strong recommendation, and that values and preferences concern how people rate outcomes, can be gathered through reviews, primary surveys or GDG experience, and call for a conditional recommendation when uncertain or variable.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-168",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Balance of benefits and harms",
      "pageStart": 138,
      "pageEnd": 138,
      "target": "../sections/sec-168-1023-balance-of-benefits-and-harms.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "When considering the balance between an intervention’s or exposure’s benefits and harms, the GDG should examine the magnitude of the effects and \nthe relative importance of the outcomes, including any disadvantages or \ninconveniences associated with the intervention (as informed by data on \nvalues and preferences). If the benefits clearly outweigh the harms, a strong \nrecommendation is likely to be issued. On the other hand, a conditional \nrecommendation is more likely if there is uncertainty about the balance of \nbenefits versus harms or when the anticipated net benefits are small.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the GDG weighs the size of benefits and harms and the relative importance of outcomes, issuing a strong recommendation when benefits clearly outweigh harms and a conditional one when the balance is uncertain or the net benefit is small.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-169",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Resource implications",
      "pageStart": 138,
      "pageEnd": 139,
      "target": "../sections/sec-169-1024-resource-implications.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Resource considerations are generally included in the formulation of recommendations, unless there is a deliberate and explicit decision to omit them. \nIn considering resource implications, the GDG can be informed by a formal \neconomic evaluation based on estimates collected during evidence retrieval \nand by modelling of cost–benefit and cost–effectiveness. If a full evaluation \nis not possible, resource implications can be anticipated and described in a \nqualitative manner. The more advantageous or disadvantageous the resource \nimplications are, the greater the likelihood of a strong recommen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that resource implications are normally included in recommendations and may be informed by formal economic evaluation, modelling or qualitative description. It says clearer advantages or disadvantages favour a strong recommendation, while uncertain costs favour a conditional one.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-170",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Priority of the problem",
      "pageStart": 139,
      "pageEnd": 139,
      "target": "../sections/sec-170-1025-priority-of-the-problem.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "GDGs should consider − and be provided with − evidence about the burden \nof disease and the baseline risk, prevalence or incidence of the problem that \nis addressed. These considerations are particularly important when the GDG \nneeds to prioritize across interventions.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that GDGs should be given evidence on burden of disease, baseline risk, prevalence or incidence, which matters especially when prioritizing across interventions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-171",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Equity and human rights",
      "pageStart": 139,
      "pageEnd": 139,
      "target": "../sections/sec-171-1026-equity-and-human-rights.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Interventions have implications for the progressive realization of the right \nto health and the options given in a guideline can reduce or increase health \ninequities. Policies or programmes that increase equity or reduce inequity \nare generally assigned higher priority by GDGs than those that do not. \nGDGs must carefully consider how the intervention might affect equity and \nhuman rights and strive to formulate recommendations in line with WHO’s \ncore principles. The greater the likelihood that the intervention will reduce \ninequities or increase equity and the greater the accessibility of an",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that GDGs must consider how an intervention affects equity and human rights, giving higher priority to those that increase equity, and that greater likelihood of reducing inequities and wider accessibility make a strong recommendation more likely.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-172",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Acceptability",
      "pageStart": 139,
      "pageEnd": 140,
      "target": "../sections/sec-172-1027-acceptability.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The lower the acceptability of an intervention to the most important stakeholders, the lower the likelihood that it will be recommended. Or, if it is recommended, the more likely it is that a strategy to address concerns about \nacceptability during implementation will be included in the guideline with \nthe recommendations. Acceptability is affected by several factors, such as who \nbenefits from an intervention and who is harmed by it; who pays for it or saves \nmoney on account of it; and when the benefits, harms and costs occur. Lack \nof acceptability may revolve around the distribution of the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that lower acceptability of an intervention to key stakeholders makes a recommendation less likely or calls for an implementation strategy to address concerns. It lists what shapes acceptability, including who benefits, is harmed or pays, timing of effects and ethical or legal issues.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-173",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Feasibility",
      "pageStart": 140,
      "pageEnd": 140,
      "target": "../sections/sec-173-1028-feasibility.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The less feasible an option is (i.e. the greater the barriers to its implementation), the lesser the likelihood that the intervention will be strongly recommended. Feasibility is influenced by the resources available, programmatic \nconsiderations, the existing and the necessary infrastructure and training, \nand many other factors.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that less feasible options, with greater implementation barriers, are less likely to be strongly recommended, and that feasibility depends on resources, programme factors, infrastructure and training.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-174",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Evidence-to-recommendation tables",
      "pageStart": 140,
      "pageEnd": 140,
      "target": "../sections/sec-174-103-evidence-to-recommendation-tables.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Evidence-to-recommendation tables depict how the factors that determine \nthe direction and strength of a recommendation inform the process of developing the recommendation. These tables enhance the transparency of the \nprocess, focus the discussions of the GDG and permit recording of the judgements made about each factor and how each one contributed to the recommendation. Because these factors overlap to some extent, the GDG should, \nat a minimum, document its judgements on the four main factors. The systematic review team and the methodologist can provide most of the information required for",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes evidence-to-recommendation tables, which show how each factor informs a recommendation, improve transparency, focus discussion and record judgements. It says the GDG should at least document the four main factors and that the review team and methodologist can prepare much of the table beforehand.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-175",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The strength of the recommendation",
      "pageStart": 140,
      "pageEnd": 141,
      "target": "../sections/sec-175-104-the-strength-of-the-recommendation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The strength of a recommendation expresses the degree to which the GDG \nis confident in the balance between the desirable and undesirable consequences of implementing the recommendation. When a GDG is very certain about this balance (i.e. the desirable consequences clearly outweigh the \nundesirable consequences), it issues a strong recommendation in favour of \nan intervention. When it is uncertain about this balance, however, it issues \na conditional (or “weak”) recommendation. Table 10.2 provides an aid to \ninterpreting the strength of a recommendation.\nChapter 10 \nDeveloping recommendations",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that recommendation strength reflects the GDG's confidence in the balance of desirable and undesirable consequences, with a strong recommendation issued when confident and a conditional (weak) one when uncertain. It refers to Table 10.2 as an interpretive aid.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-176",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Strong recommendations",
      "pageStart": 141,
      "pageEnd": 141,
      "target": "../sections/sec-176-1041-strong-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Strong recommendations communicate the message that the guideline is \nbased on the confidence that the desirable effects of adherence to the recommendation outweigh the undesirable consequences. Strong recommendations are uncommon because the balance between the benefits and harms \nof implementing a recommendation is rarely certain. In particular, GDGs \nneed to be cautious when considering making strong recommendations on \nthe basis of evidence whose quality is low or very low.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that strong recommendations convey confidence that desirable effects outweigh undesirable ones, are uncommon because the balance is rarely certain, and call for caution when evidence quality is low or very low.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-177",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Conditional or weak recommendations",
      "pageStart": 141,
      "pageEnd": 142,
      "target": "../sections/sec-177-1042-conditional-or-weak-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Recommendations that are conditional or weak are made when a GDG is less \ncertain about the balance between the benefits and harms or disadvantages \nof implementing a recommendation. Conditional recommendations generally include a description of the conditions under which the end-user should \nor should not implement the recommendation.\nTable 10.2.\t Interpretation of strong and conditional recommendations \nfor an intervention\nAudience\nStrong recommendation\nConditional recommendation\nPatients\nMost individuals in this situation would want the \nrecommended course of action; only a small proportion",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that conditional (weak) recommendations are made when the guideline group is less certain about the balance of benefits and harms, and that they usually state the conditions for implementing them. Includes a table interpreting strong versus conditional recommendations for patients, clinicians and policy-makers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-178",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formulating recommendations",
      "pageStart": 142,
      "pageEnd": 142,
      "target": "../sections/sec-178-105-formulating-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The evidence-to-recommendation tables depict not only the evidence and \njudgements leading to a recommendation, but also the justifications for the \nrecommendation’s direction and strength. They also describe the subgroups \nconsidered, the process used to formulate recommendations (e.g. if voting \ntook place) and key issues surrounding implementation, evaluation and \nmonitoring (see Chapter 13) as well as research gaps.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how evidence-to-recommendation tables record the evidence, judgements and justification for a recommendation's direction and strength, along with subgroups, the formulation process, implementation issues and research gaps.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-179",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Reaching agreement on recommendations",
      "pageStart": 142,
      "pageEnd": 142,
      "target": "../sections/sec-179-1051-reaching-agreement-on-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Draft recommendations can be prepared either by the steering group \nbefore the GDG meets to formulate recommendations, or during the meeting by the GDG itself. Under the leadership of the chair, the GDG reviews \nand discusses the GRADE evidence profiles presented by a representative \nof the systematic review team. In addition, the GDG considers other relevant criteria and evidence as discussed above. If the steering group has \nnot drafted the evidence-to-decision tables before the GDG meeting, the \nGDG assesses each of the factors in Table 10.1 at its meeting and completes \nthe evidence-to-dec",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how draft recommendations are prepared by the steering group or the guideline group and reviewed against GRADE evidence profiles and evidence-to-decision tables at the meeting. States that the group must agree on direction and strength by consensus, and that strong disagreement points to a conditional recommendation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-180",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Writing recommendations",
      "pageStart": 142,
      "pageEnd": 143,
      "target": "../sections/sec-180-106-writing-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Recommendations need to be clear and actionable, reflect the PICO format \nand contain an indication of their strength and of the quality of the evidence \non which they are based. Outcomes should generally not be mentioned in \nthe recommendation to avoid the impression that only single outcomes are \nrelevant or were considered.\nThe language of each recommendation is critically important. Wherever possible, it should be consistent across all recommendations in a guideline, which \nshould be written in the active voice. GRADE recommends using terms or phrases \nChapter 10 \nDeveloping recommendation",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out how recommendations should be written: clear, actionable, in PICO format, in the active voice, with consistent wording and an indication of strength and evidence quality. Advises on terms such as should and suggest, prefers explicit wording like recommending against over the ambiguous not recommended, and says each recommendation needs a justification, remarks on context and links to its supporting evidence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-181",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "When not to make recommendations",
      "pageStart": 143,
      "pageEnd": 144,
      "target": "../sections/sec-181-107-when-not-to-make-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In rare situations, a GDG may decide that the evidence is not sufficient to be \nable to formulate a recommendation. For instance, it may not be appropriate \nto make a recommendation when no evidence about the effects of an intervention is found, in which case the following statement could be included: \n“No recommendation can be made because evidence on the effectiveness (or \nharms) of intervention X was not identified.”\nInstead of providing a recommendation, it is sometimes appropriate to \npublish the results of the systematic review showing a lack of studies, or \nan overview of the available",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that a guideline group may occasionally decide the evidence is insufficient to make a recommendation, and gives a model statement for such cases. Stresses that this should be exceptional, that the lack of evidence must be stated openly, and that case reports or national experience can instead support a documented conditional recommendation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-182",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Future research",
      "pageStart": 144,
      "pageEnd": 144,
      "target": "../sections/sec-182-108-future-research.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "When gaps in the evidence are such that significant uncertainty exists with \nrespect to the balance of an intervention’s benefits and harms, such knowledge gaps should be described and questions and methods for addressing the \ngaps should be suggested. The research agenda can be prioritized, if appropriate. This section of the guideline document will inform future recommendations and guidelines. In formulating questions for future research, \nthe GDG should be as specific as possible about what is needed and why. The \nPICO framework is also very useful for this purpose.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that significant evidence gaps should be described in the guideline with suggested questions and methods for future research, possibly prioritized and framed using PICO.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-183",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "References",
      "pageStart": 144,
      "pageEnd": 145,
      "target": "../sections/sec-183-109-references.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1.\t\nAndrews J, Guyatt G, Oxman AD, Alderson P, Dahm P, Falck-Ytter Y, et al. GRADE guidelines: 14. Going from evidence to recommendations: the significance and presentation of \nrecommendations. J Clin Epidemiol. 2013;66(7):719–25. doi: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.\njclinepi.2012.03.013 PMID: 23312392\n2.\t\nGuyatt GH, Oxman AD, Kunz R, Falck-Ytter Y, Vist GE, Liberati A, et al.; GRADE Working \nGroup. Going from evidence to recommendations. BMJ. 2008;336(7652):1049–51. doi: \nhttp://dx.doi.org/10.1136/bmj.39493.646875.AE PMID: 18467413\n3.\t\nGuyatt GH, Oxman AD, Kunz R, Jaeschke R, Helfand M, Liberati",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A reference list of publications cited in the chapter on developing recommendations, mostly on GRADE and moving from evidence to recommendations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-184",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is a rapid advice guideline?",
      "pageStart": 145,
      "pageEnd": 145,
      "target": "../sections/sec-184-111-what-is-a-rapid-advice-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In response to a public health emergency, WHO must provide global leadership \nand timely guidance in the form of an evidence-informed guideline produced \nwithin one to three months. The steering group for such a rapid advice guideline must follow all of the basic steps for guideline development as described in \nthis handbook, but with modifications to meet the accelerated timeline.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the rapid advice guideline as an evidence-informed guideline produced within one to three months in response to a public health emergency, following the usual steps with modifications for the accelerated timeline.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-185",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is a rapid review?",
      "pageStart": 145,
      "pageEnd": 146,
      "target": "../sections/sec-185-112-what-is-a-rapid-review.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Because recommendations issued by WHO need to be based on the best \navailable evidence, guidelines typically draw on evidence from well conducted and reported systematic reviews, whether they exist already or are \nundertaken to inform the development of the guideline. Producing systematic reviews can take varying amounts of time – from six months to more \nthan one year − depending on the complexity of the topic and the available resources. In a public health emergency, conducting a conventional or \nstandard systematic review de novo may not be feasible. Rapid reviews have \nemerged as a streaml",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a rapid review as an evidence review using accelerated or modified systematic review methods, explaining that a standard systematic review can take six months to over a year and may be infeasible in an emergency. States that rapid reviews keep core values such as transparency and bias minimization, and lists efficiencies such as narrower scope, fewer outcomes, more reviewers in parallel and streamlined processes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-186",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the evidence on the validity and impact of rapid reviews and rapid advice guidelines?",
      "pageStart": 146,
      "pageEnd": 146,
      "target": "../sections/sec-186-113-what-is-the-evidence-on-the-validity-and-imp.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "rapid reviews and rapid advice guidelines?\nTo date limited guidance has been provided on when and how to conduct \na rapid review, and few data exist on how a rapid review performs compared with a standard systematic review. Available data do point to wide \nand increasing interest − and great variability − in the approaches used and \nlevel of reporting of the methods and results across organizations producing rapid reviews and rapid advice guidelines (2–4). Rapid reviews may be \nnarrower in scope and more tailored to the needs of the commissioning \nbody and intended users than standard systemat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that little guidance and few data exist on how rapid reviews perform against standard systematic reviews, and that approaches and reporting vary widely across organizations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-187",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is a rapid advice guideline needed?",
      "pageStart": 146,
      "pageEnd": 147,
      "target": "../sections/sec-187-114-is-a-rapid-advice-guideline-needed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The first and most important question to consider is whether a rapid advice \nguideline is appropriate in the setting of the public health emergency at hand. \nChapter 11 \nRapid advice guidelines in the setting of a public health emergency\n135\n135\nThe need for, and appropriateness of, undertaking a rapid advice guideline, \nin light of the potential limitations of this approach, warrant consideration \nin the initial planning stage of a rapid advice guideline. Several issues need \nto be considered when deciding whether to develop a rapid advice guideline instead of a standard guideline, or to defe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the first question is whether a rapid advice guideline is appropriate in the emergency, as opposed to a standard guideline or deferring development, and that this should be considered at the planning stage.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-188",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the type of emergency and the risk to public health?",
      "pageStart": 147,
      "pageEnd": 147,
      "target": "../sections/sec-188-1141-what-is-the-type-of-emergency-and-the-risk.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "health?\nThe first step is to examine the public health event that is driving the request \nfor a rapid advice guideline. Emergencies may be classified as natural, technological, or conflict-related and may be of sudden onset (e.g. earthquakes, tsunamis, chemical crises) or more gradual onset (e.g. deteriorating situations in \narmed conflict, progressive disease outbreaks, drought or food insecurities). \nAll types of emergencies can evolve into prolonged or protracted situations.\nWHO and Member States use the manual entitled Rapid risk assessment \nof acute public health events to assess “any out",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to examine the type of emergency driving the request, classing emergencies as natural, technological or conflict-related and sudden or gradual. Refers to WHO's rapid risk assessment manual and the Emergency Response Framework, including the grading of emergencies from Grade 1 to Grade 3.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-189",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Is the event novel?",
      "pageStart": 147,
      "pageEnd": 148,
      "target": "../sections/sec-189-1142-is-the-event-novel.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO staff may consider producing a rapid advice guideline primarily in the \nface of one of two types of events:\n■■\na new situation (e.g. a new strain of influenza; the Middle East respiratory syndrome [MERS] coronavirus; or an earthquake); or\n■■\nan event encountered previously but causing problems in a different \ncontext (e.g. a natural disaster; a change in disease pattern such as the \nEbola virus disease in West Africa in 2014; or a prolonged armed conflict compounded by a disease outbreak).\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n136\n136\nIf the event is not novel, high-quality guidelines tha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that a rapid advice guideline is mainly considered for a new situation or a known event in a new context, and that for non-novel events suitable high-quality guidelines may already exist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-190",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Does uncertainty need to be urgently addressed?",
      "pageStart": 148,
      "pageEnd": 148,
      "target": "../sections/sec-190-1143-does-uncertainty-need-to-be-urgently-addres.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Guidelines are indicated when there is uncertainty about what to do in a specific situation. Rapid advice guidelines are no different. WHO staff may be \nuncertain about what advice to provide, or there may be uncertainty in the \nfield, with different players having different viewpoints and approaches. The \nquestion here is how quickly the uncertainty needs to be addressed.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that rapid advice guidelines, like others, address uncertainty about what to do, and that the question is how quickly it must be resolved.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-191",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is the anticipated time frame for the event?",
      "pageStart": 148,
      "pageEnd": 148,
      "target": "../sections/sec-191-1144-what-is-the-anticipated-time-frame-for-the.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The intent of rapid advice guidelines is to provide urgently needed, evidence-\ninformed recommendations that can be implemented within one to three months \nof the appearance of an unfolding situation or crisis. If from the outset an event \nis judged likely to persist beyond six months, a rapid advice guideline may not \nbe suitable. It is important to weigh the impact of developing recommendations \nusing standard processes and timelines, rather than produce a guideline that \nmay be prone to serious limitations under an accelerated timeline.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that rapid advice guidelines aim to give recommendations implementable within one to three months, and may be unsuitable if the event is expected to last beyond six months.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-192",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Will the rapid advice guideline be rapidly implemented?",
      "pageStart": 148,
      "pageEnd": 149,
      "target": "../sections/sec-192-1145-will-the-rapid-advice-guideline-be-rapidly.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Rapid advice guidelines should only be developed if a mechanism is in place \nfor disseminating and implementing them, and if implementing the recommendations is feasible in the context of the emergency. Various factors need \nto be carefully considered before embarking on the guideline development \nprocess. They include the existence of health systems and other infrastructure, the acceptability of the proposed intervention, the training requirements involved, and resource availability.\nIn summary, the decision to develop a rapid advice guideline should be \ninformed by the above-mentioned factor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says a rapid advice guideline should be developed only if dissemination and implementation mechanisms exist and are feasible, considering health systems, acceptability, training and resources. Summarizes the conditions for going ahead: a clear need, manageable scope, adequate expertise and funding, and an implementation plan.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-193",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How is a rapid advice guideline developed?",
      "pageStart": 149,
      "pageEnd": 149,
      "target": "../sections/sec-193-115-how-is-a-rapid-advice-guideline-developed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The basic steps for developing a rapid advice guideline are identical to those \nthat apply to standard guidelines, as outlined in Chapter 1. There are some \ndifferences and additional considerations, however, when developing a rapid \nadvice guideline: the steps involved are depicted in Table 11.1.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the development of rapid advice guidelines as following the same basic steps as standard guidelines, with differences summarized in a table.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-194",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Consult the GRC Secretariat early",
      "pageStart": 149,
      "pageEnd": 149,
      "target": "../sections/sec-194-1151-consult-the-grc-secretariat-early.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "It is particularly important for the responsible technical officer of a rapid \nadvice guideline to contact the GRC Secretariat early in the guideline development process. The GRC and the Secretariat will provide guidance as to \nwhether a rapid advice guideline is appropriate in the situation at hand. In \naddition, the Secretariat can assist in developing the planning proposal and \nidentifying a review team and a guideline methodologist. The GRC needs \nto approve the development of a rapid advice guideline at the planning proposal stage, or else the final guideline cannot be reviewed and assess",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises the responsible technical officer to contact the Guideline Review Committee Secretariat early for guidance, help with the planning proposal and review team, and notes the committee must approve development at the planning stage for accelerated review.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-195",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formulate the various groups involved in guideline development",
      "pageStart": 149,
      "pageEnd": 152,
      "target": "../sections/sec-195-1152-formulate-the-various-groups-involved-in-gu.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "development\nTo determine the most appropriate scope and key questions, the steering \ngroup needs to be quickly assembled and all members fully engaged in the \nguideline development process. The chair and other potential members of \nthe GDG should also be identified early and approached regarding their \ninterest and availability within the set time frame. Ideally one or more key \nmembers of the GDG should be available immediately to provide feedback \non the proposed scope and key questions. If the draft final guideline is to \nundergo peer review, potential reviewers − individuals or organizatio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to assemble the steering group, guideline development group and peer reviewers quickly, including expertise in ethical, social, legal, equity, gender and human rights issues. Presents a table of the steps of rapid advice guideline development across planning, development, and publishing and updating phases, naming who is responsible and the key points that differ from standard guidelines. Notes that human rights and gender issues often arise early in a response.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-196",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Scope the rapid advice guideline and define the key questions",
      "pageStart": 152,
      "pageEnd": 152,
      "target": "../sections/sec-196-1153-scope-the-rapid-advice-guideline-and-define.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "questions\nOnce the need for a rapid advice guideline has been established and at least \nthe core members of the steering group have been identified, work should \nbegin on defining the scope of the guideline and on developing the key questions in PICO format. A rapid advice guideline will most likely provide recommendations on the benefits and harms of interventions: it is less likely to \ndeal with matters concerning diagnosis, prognosis or risk thresholds.\nWHO staff, with the assistance of an information specialist, should perform a rapid scoping exercise to provide a general sense of the exte",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to define the scope and PICO key questions once the need is established, noting such guidelines mostly address benefits and harms of interventions. Describes a rapid scoping search of sources such as MEDLINE, the Cochrane Library and existing WHO guidance, limited to one or two days and summarized in the planning proposal.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-197",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Prepare the planning proposal for the GRC",
      "pageStart": 152,
      "pageEnd": 153,
      "target": "../sections/sec-197-1154-prepare-the-planning-proposal-for-the-grc.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Like standard guidelines, rapid advice guidelines require that a planning proposal be submitted to the GRC for review and disposition. The content, level \nof detail and format are the same as those outlined in Chapter 4. Although \nthe planning proposal takes time to prepare, it is nonetheless essential for \nthe development of a rapid advice guideline, since it serves as a point of reference for the steering group, the systematic review team and the guideline \nmethodologist. The planning proposal describes both the processes and procedures to be used to develop the guideline and provides a deta",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that a rapid advice guideline still needs a planning proposal submitted to the review committee, in the same format as for standard guidelines. Says it should be detailed, kept up to date as a living document with rationale for changes, because the rapid review process is more fluid and documentation supports transparency.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-198",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How do rapid reviews compare with systematic reviews?",
      "pageStart": 153,
      "pageEnd": 153,
      "target": "../sections/sec-198-116-how-do-rapid-reviews-compare-with-systematic.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "reviews?\nThe core principles of evidence searching and retrieval, including transparency and reproducible and explicit methods, apply to rapid reviews. There are \nkey differences, however, between searching in the context of a rapid review \nand of a standard systematic review. The rapid review has more restricted \nsearch criteria; looks to existing high-quality systematic reviews as the first \nline of evidence; involves a more targeted and iterative procedure for screening the literature and for data analysis and synthesis; places less emphasis on \nmeta-analyses; and involves a concise and abb",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Contrasts rapid with standard systematic reviews: restricted search criteria, existing systematic reviews as first evidence, targeted iterative screening, less emphasis on meta-analysis and shorter reports, while keeping transparency and explicit methods.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-199",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Types of rapid reviews",
      "pageStart": 153,
      "pageEnd": 155,
      "target": "../sections/sec-199-1161-types-of-rapid-reviews.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The reviews that underpin rapid advice guidelines may be categorized into \nfour basic types: a full, standard systematic review performed rapidly; a \nreview involving a variety of abbreviated methods and including only systematic reviews and existing guidelines; a review of primary studies and \nexisting systematic reviews, or a review of primary studies only. Fig 11.1 \noutlines the types of rapid reviews, along with their similarities and distinguishing features.\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n142\n142\nFig. 11.1.\t\nTypes of rapid review and their characteristics\nGRADE: Grading of Recomme",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes four types of rapid review, from a full systematic review done quickly to reviews of only systematic reviews and guidelines, or of primary studies, and refers to a figure outlining their features.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/figure-img-p154-1",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Figure",
        "doco:Figure"
      ],
      "kind": "figure",
      "title": "",
      "pageStart": 154,
      "pageEnd": 154,
      "target": "../images/img-p154-1.png",
      "narrative": "draft",
      "content": "A comparison of four types of rapid review — a traditional systematic review conducted rapidly, a rapid review of systematic reviews, a rapid review of systematic reviews plus primary studies, and a rapid review of primary studies only — across twelve methodological dimensions. The traditional review allows up to 16 weeks where the three rapid forms allow up to 12. It differs from them throughout: no restrictions on literature search or number of databases against restrictions and 2–3 databases; complete data-extraction verification against one reviewer with selected verification; outcomes restricted to four or fewer against two to four with more if available. Rows also cover question types, number of questions, use of systematic reviews, grey literature, screening, study designs included, risk-of-bias assessment, and assessment of the quality of the body of evidence.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": null
    },
    {
