    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-201",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Select the types of evidence to be collected and identify the appropriate sources",
      "pageStart": 155,
      "pageEnd": 155,
      "target": "../sections/sec-201-1171-select-the-types-of-evidence-to-be-collecte.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "the appropriate sources\nDepending on the nature of the question being asked, the purpose of the \nrapid review and the magnitude of the literature on the topic, various types \nof evidence may be targeted. In most cases the emphasis will be placed on \nlocating and summarizing evidence from relevant and high-quality “off-the-\nshelf’ systematic reviews or guidelines. In the absence of systematic reviews, \nhigh-quality and/or recent primary studies may also be included. Landmark \npapers may also be included for reference. Finally, quasi-experimental and/\nor observational studies of high quality may",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains which evidence to target and where to look, emphasizing existing high-quality systematic reviews and guidelines, then primary studies, landmark papers and good observational studies as needed. Notes that rapid reviews usually search only two to three key databases.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-202",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Develop search strategies",
      "pageStart": 155,
      "pageEnd": 156,
      "target": "../sections/sec-202-1172-develop-search-strategies.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Concepts need to be well defined with search terms that include both medical subject headings (MeSH) and text words. It is important that the draft \nsearch strategy be reviewed by:\n■■\nat least one other member of the rapid review team;\n■■\none or more content experts, such as WHO technical staff; and\n■■\na WHO information specialist with expertise in systematic reviews.\nValidated search filters may be very useful when performing a rapid \nreview (see Chapter 8). The search filters of most interest are those that comprise index terms relating to study type and design, such as randomized \ncontrolle",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to build rapid review search strategies, using subject headings and text words, with review by a team member, content experts and an information specialist. Notes that study-design filters can surface top evidence first and that a rapid review may favour precision over recall, with the choice reported.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-203",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Search for grey literature",
      "pageStart": 156,
      "pageEnd": 156,
      "target": "../sections/sec-203-1173-search-for-grey-literature.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A search for grey literature should be considered but needs to be limited. For \nexample, the websites of relevant organizations can quickly provide relevant \ndata. An information specialist at WHO’s headquarters library should be \nconsulted regarding the proposed strategy for searching the grey literature.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises that a limited grey literature search, such as checking relevant organizations' websites, may be considered, in consultation with a WHO library information specialist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-204",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Common search restrictions",
      "pageStart": 156,
      "pageEnd": 157,
      "target": "../sections/sec-204-1174-common-search-restrictions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Several common eligibility restrictions should be considered when developing search strategies in the context of a rapid review (Box 11.1) (1). Potential \nrestrictions should be discussed among steering group members and with \nthe review team information specialist to optimally balance precision and \nrecall of the search strategies.\nSearch strategies for a rapid review will generally have language restrictions, since translation is time-consuming. The languages of inclusion \nshould be carefully selected based on the guideline topic. For example, a \nrapid review on personal protective equipment",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses common eligibility restrictions for rapid review searches, especially language limits chosen by topic, illustrated by an Ebola protective equipment review limited to English and French. Says restrictions must be documented in the proposal, report and guideline, and that titles in excluded languages should be listed in an appendix.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-205",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Other strategies for identifying relevant literature",
      "pageStart": 157,
      "pageEnd": 158,
      "target": "../sections/sec-205-1175-other-strategies-for-identifying-relevant-l.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In the context of a new situation or event, the best (and perhaps only) data \nmight come from the acquisition and analysis of emerging information in real \ntime. In the Ebola virus disease outbreak in West Africa in 2014, essentially no \nrelevant comparative data were obtained through a systematic review of the \npublished literature. WHO staff therefore rapidly devised and implemented \na survey of repatriated health-care workers to gather information on these \nworkers’ experiences with various types of personal protective equipment.\nBox 11.1.\t\nCommon search restrictions for rapid reviews\nSourc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes other ways to find evidence, such as real-time data collection like the survey of repatriated health workers during the 2014 Ebola outbreak, and scanning reference lists. Includes a box of common search restrictions covering sources, language, accessible studies, grey literature, dates and region.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-206",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Screening and study selection",
      "pageStart": 158,
      "pageEnd": 158,
      "target": "../sections/sec-206-1176-screening-and-study-selection.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Standard systematic review methods apply to the process of screening the \nrecords retrieved from bibliographic databases and other searches. Records \nshould be imported into reference management software in the usual manner \nto facilitate record management and citation screening and management \nduring the drafting of the guideline document.\nAs for a standard systematic review, study selection involves a two-step \nprocess. First, two people independently screen titles and abstracts and all \npotentially relevant records, as well as records lacking enough information \nto determine their eligibili",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes screening and study selection for rapid reviews as following standard methods, with two people independently screening titles and abstracts and two reviewers assessing full texts, reaching consensus with a third reviewer if needed. Notes that one reviewer plus a check of exclusions is an acceptable alternative at the first step.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-207",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Use a stepwise approach with emphasis on higher levels of evidence",
      "pageStart": 158,
      "pageEnd": 158,
      "target": "../sections/sec-207-1177-use-a-stepwise-approach-with-emphasis-on-hi.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "evidence\nTo keep the scope of the rapid reviews within the bounds dictated by timelines and resources, initially the evidence is often limited to what is found in \nsystematic reviews. If primary studies, such as RCTs, will be included, this \nneeds to be justified in the planning proposal and reflected in the timelines \nand budget. Further restrictions (e.g. by outcomes or study quality criteria) may be considered to accommodate the inclusion of primary studies. \nExcluding studies that do not report the outcomes of interest may lead to bias \nfrom selective outcome reporting. However, this is a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends starting with evidence from systematic reviews and adding primary studies only when justified in the planning proposal and reflected in timelines and budget. Acknowledges that restricting by outcomes risks selective reporting bias but is a trade-off under time limits.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-208",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Obtaining publications",
      "pageStart": 158,
      "pageEnd": 159,
      "target": "../sections/sec-208-1178-obtaining-publications.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Records that are not available electronically are generally excluded because \nthe timeline of a rapid advice guideline is not compatible with the delays \nChapter 11 \nRapid advice guidelines in the setting of a public health emergency\n147\ninvolved in inter-library loans. WHO information scientists should be contacted before concluding that a given publication is not available, since \nWHO may have ready access to the information. Even if the full text cannot \nbe obtained or translated, the abstract may provide valuable information, \nparticularly when the evidence is sparse.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that records not available electronically are generally excluded because of time, though WHO information scientists should be consulted first and abstracts may still help when evidence is sparse.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-209",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data extraction and evidence synthesis",
      "pageStart": 159,
      "pageEnd": 159,
      "target": "../sections/sec-209-1179-data-extraction-and-evidence-synthesis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Before starting data extraction, it is important to carefully consider what \nspecific information the GDG will need to make its decisions so that only \nessential information is extracted. A standard extraction form should be \ndeveloped to facilitate accurate and reliable data collection, and it should be \npilot-tested. One reviewer usually extracts the data and a second one verifies \neach record. If full verification is not feasible, a random sample of at least \n10% of the included studies should be independently checked to provide \nsome measure of quality assurance. If inter-rater agreement i",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes data extraction for rapid reviews: extract only information the guideline group needs, use a pilot-tested form, and have a second reviewer verify, or check a random sample of at least 10 percent.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-210",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assess the risk of bias at the individual study level",
      "pageStart": 159,
      "pageEnd": 160,
      "target": "../sections/sec-210-11710-assess-the-risk-of-bias-at-the-individual.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The review team should assess the risk of bias for individual studies, just as in \na standard systematic review. This step is necessary to properly interpret the \nresults of a rapid review and to use GRADE to assess the strength of the body \nof evidence for each outcome. For rapid reviews particularly, the assessment \nof the risk of bias may be used to select the studies included in the review, \nonce initial criteria based on study design have been applied.\n11.7.11 Evidence synthesis\nThe rapid review report includes, first, a summary of the overall findings of \nthe literature search and of the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that risk of bias should be assessed for individual studies and may help select studies for inclusion. Describes evidence synthesis for rapid reviews, with a PRISMA flow diagram, a narrative summary organized by PICO, stepwise extraction of effect estimates, outcome-specific synthesis, and meta-analysis only if time allows while reporting existing meta-analyses.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-211",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assessing the quality of the body of evidence using GRADE",
      "pageStart": 160,
      "pageEnd": 162,
      "target": "../sections/sec-211-11712-assessing-the-quality-of-the-body-of-evide.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "GRADE\nThe quality of the body of evidence pertaining to each outcome should be \nassessed using GRADE, as appropriate for the type of data involved (see \nChapter 9). The focus is on outcomes critical for decision-making in the context of rapid advice guidelines, and not on intermediate, surrogate or other \ntypes of outcomes. Exceptions may be made, however, when data are sparse \nand decisions need to be based on indirect evidence, including intermediate \noutcomes, for example.\nAssessments of the quality of the evidence using GRADE are not \nrestricted to cases in which pooled estimates of effect",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the quality of evidence for each outcome should be assessed with GRADE, focusing on critical outcomes, and that narrative syntheses can also be graded. Notes that modifications to standard methods can be listed under other considerations, and includes a figure on four approaches to rapid review of evidence, from summarizing one high-quality systematic review to updating one or adding primary studies.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-212",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The rapid review report",
      "pageStart": 162,
      "pageEnd": 163,
      "target": "../sections/sec-212-118-the-rapid-review-report.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The review team needs to produce a concise report that succinctly yet methodically summarizes the methods used and the results of the review. Suggested \ncomponents of the rapid review report are listed in Box 11.2. All components \nshould be described briefly; excessive detail is not necessary. The rapid review \nmethods should be reported at a level of detail that will allow them to be replicated. A brief section on the gaps in the evidence and future research needs \nmay be very useful and particularly important when data are sparse. A written disclosure should be provided to the effect that th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the concise rapid review report, which should allow replication, note evidence gaps and carry a disclosure that it is not a gold standard systematic review. Lists suggested components in a box covering introduction, methods, results, discussion, information page, references and appendices.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-213",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formulate recommendations",
      "pageStart": 163,
      "pageEnd": 163,
      "target": "../sections/sec-213-119-formulate-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "empty"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-214",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Convene the meeting of the guideline development group",
      "pageStart": 163,
      "pageEnd": 164,
      "target": "../sections/sec-214-1191-convene-the-meeting-of-the-guideline-develo.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "At the very beginning of the guideline development process, the responsible \ntechnical officer and the steering group need to plan for the GDG meeting where recommendations will be formulated. Because of the compressed \ntimeline, administrative details need to be addressed early. This includes \nidentifying a meeting space, issuing invitations to potential GDG members, \ncollecting declarations of interest and managing conflicts of interest, and \n... continued\nDiscussion\n■■\nThe strengths and limitations of the review process, focusing particularly on how \nthe methods differed from those of a sta",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the meeting of the guideline development group must be planned from the start, covering venue, invitations, declarations of interest and travel, which needs dedicated administrative support. Notes that virtual meetings are possible but an in-person meeting is generally preferable.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-215",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Evidence to recommendations",
      "pageStart": 164,
      "pageEnd": 164,
      "target": "../sections/sec-215-1192-evidence-to-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The GRADE approach for formulating recommendations should be followed \nwhen developing rapid advice guidelines (see Chapter 10). The tools used \nto translate evidence into recommendations are equally applicable to rapid \nadvice and standard guidelines. In the context of a rapid advice guideline, it \nwill seldom be feasible to collect primary data or to perform a review of the \nvalues and preferences surrounding the outcomes of interest in the guideline, nor will there be time to collect detailed information on resource use. \nHowever, data that can be readily obtained should be collected (e.g.",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that the GRADE approach and its tools apply to rapid advice guidelines, noting it is seldom feasible to collect primary data on values, preferences or resource use, though readily available data such as costs should be gathered.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-216",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Draft the guideline document",
      "pageStart": 164,
      "pageEnd": 164,
      "target": "../sections/sec-216-1110-draft-the-guideline-document.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The process and resources needed to draft the final rapid advice guideline document are essentially the same as for standard guidelines. The writer should \nbe identified early and in most situations this will be the responsible technical officer or other member(s) of the steering group. If an external contractor \nis used, he or she should be identified near the beginning of the guideline \ndevelopment process and they must be involved in all stages of the process.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the drafting of a rapid advice guideline needs the same process and resources as a standard one, with the writer, usually the technical officer or steering group member, or a contractor, identified early.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-217",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "External peer review",
      "pageStart": 164,
      "pageEnd": 165,
      "target": "../sections/sec-217-1111-external-peer-review.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Peer review of the draft of the final guideline document by key individuals, \nboth internal and external to WHO, is important for all guidelines produced by WHO, including those produced rapidly. In the context of a rapid \nChapter 11 \nRapid advice guidelines in the setting of a public health emergency\n153\n153\nadvice guideline, peer review will be accelerated and abbreviated. Early in \nthe guideline development process, the steering group should identify three \nto six key individuals and solicit their interest, availability and commitment \nto a very short turn-around time for their reviews. Lea",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that rapid advice guidelines still need accelerated, abbreviated peer review by three to six key individuals and leading external organizations, perhaps with 48 to 72 hours to respond. Says that in rare cases no peer review is possible, in which case all relevant WHO departments must at least be able to give input.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-218",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Implementation and the importance of context",
      "pageStart": 165,
      "pageEnd": 165,
      "target": "../sections/sec-218-1112-implementation-and-the-importance-of-contex.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Most evidence stemming from research is generated in settings and populations that differ from those affected by a public health emergency. Thus, the \ndegree to which such evidence may be directly applied to the current context may be limited. It is important to consider how contextual factors can \nmodify the benefits and harms of an intervention, and how various barriers \nand facilitators can affect implementation and impact. Therefore, in a rapid \nreview that relies heavily on evidence from systematic reviews, the synthesis \nshould be tailored to the local context throughout all stages of th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that research evidence often comes from settings unlike the emergency, so contextual factors, barriers and facilitators must be considered, the synthesis tailored to the local context, and decision-makers given what they need to apply it.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-219",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Publishing",
      "pageStart": 165,
      "pageEnd": 166,
      "target": "../sections/sec-219-1113-publishing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Preparing the final guideline document for publication involves the same \nsteps as described for a standard guideline. Electronic means will usually be \nused for initial dissemination, followed by print circulation as required in \nthe local context (see Chapter 12 and Chapter 13).\nWHO handbook for guideline development\n154\n154",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that publishing a rapid advice guideline follows the same steps as for a standard guideline, with electronic dissemination first and print as needed locally.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-220",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Updating",
      "pageStart": 166,
      "pageEnd": 166,
      "target": "../sections/sec-220-1114-updating.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "All guidelines need to be kept up to date and consistent with the best available evidence. This is particularly important and difficult to achieve in the \ncontext of a public health emergency, when new data are constantly emerging and experience is continually accruing. The technical unit with primary \nresponsibility for the rapid advice guideline must keep abreast of new information and continually assess how such information might affect the recommendations given in the guideline. If data emerge to suggest that the \ncurrent recommendations need to be revised, WHO needs to be prepared to \nund",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that rapid advice guidelines must be kept up to date as new data emerge, and that their short lifespan and review-by date should be shown prominently. Discusses deciding when to replace one with a standard guideline, using the 2009 H1N1 guidance on pregnant women as an example, and notes WHO may sponsor systematic reviews to confirm high-impact recommendations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-221",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guideline Review Committee processes for rapid advice guidelines",
      "pageStart": 166,
      "pageEnd": 167,
      "target": "../sections/sec-221-1115-guideline-review-committee-processes-for-ra.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "advice guidelines\nThe GRC needs to approve the development of a rapid advice guideline. The \nappropriateness of developing a guideline in the context of a specific public \nChapter 11 \nRapid advice guidelines in the setting of a public health emergency\n155\nhealth emergency is considered when the technical unit contacts the GRC \nSecretariat; it is formally addressed when the GRC reviews the planning proposal. The planning proposal must provide adequate justification for applying an accelerated and abbreviated process; prolonged emergencies that call \nfor a rapid advance guideline are uncommon.\nT",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the Guideline Review Committee's role: it must approve development of a rapid advice guideline, judging appropriateness through the planning proposal, and review the final guideline. Says the handbook's principles still apply with case-by-case exceptions, and that the committee provides expedited reviews and ad hoc meetings.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-222",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Conclusions",
      "pageStart": 167,
      "pageEnd": 168,
      "target": "../sections/sec-222-1116-conclusions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This chapter outlines the processes and methods used to develop a rapid \nadvice guideline within one to three months in the context of a public health \nemergency. The development of a rapid advance guideline differs in important ways from the development of a standard guideline. Unlike a standard \nguideline, a rapid advice guideline must have a very narrow scope to make \ndevelopment feasible within the given time frame. Moreover, WHO staff \nand external experts must be identified and engaged from the very beginning of the guideline development process, and the GRC Secretariat must \nbe contacte",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes that rapid advice guidelines are developed in one to three months with a very narrow scope, early engagement of experts and the review committee Secretariat, and more fluid evidence review methods. Stresses that core principles of minimizing bias, transparency and meeting audience needs still apply, and that limitations and a commitment to update must be stated.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-223",
