    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-225",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guideline format",
      "pageStart": 169,
      "pageEnd": 171,
      "target": "../sections/sec-225-121-guideline-format.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "All guidelines should have an executive summary, a main body and appendices. The executive summary should contain the key recommendations of the \nguideline. As executive summaries are often read as stand-alone documents, \nthe strength of the recommendation and the quality of the evidence for each \nrecommendation should be specified in the executive summary, as well as in \nthe main body of the guideline. Authors may wish to note contextual issues \nfor each recommendation and include a brief methods section and citations, \nalthough these are not required.\nThe main text of the guideline should in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the required guideline format: an executive summary with key recommendations, strength and evidence quality, a main body with introduction, methods, recommendations, conclusions and participant and interest details, and appendices for supporting documentation. Also covers use of PRISMA and the AGREE-II appraisal before clearance, and begins the peer review section, stating that WHO guidelines must undergo peer review by the external review group and WHO departments, with changes limited at that stage. Text under this heading therefore extends into the next section.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-226",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Producing and publishing the guideline",
      "pageStart": 171,
      "pageEnd": 171,
      "target": "../sections/sec-226-12-producing-and-publishing-the-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "159\n159\n■■\nSystematic review protocols outlining the search strategy, study eligibility criteria and research synthesis methods can be circulated to \nmembers of the external review group for comments.\n■■\nThe list of included studies identified by searching bibliographic databases \ncan be sent to technical experts to look for studies that have been missed.\n■■\nDraft evidence profiles can be circulated to experts, again for the identification of missing data.\nAll WHO guidelines require some form of external peer review of the \ndraft final guideline. Because of the time constraints in the developm",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists ways to involve external reviewers earlier, such as circulating systematic review protocols, lists of included studies and draft evidence profiles, and states that all guidelines need external peer review, with a few reviewers and short turn-around for rapid advice guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-227",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The production process",
      "pageStart": 171,
      "pageEnd": 171,
      "target": "../sections/sec-227-123-the-production-process.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Production of WHO guidelines should follow the same process as for other \nWHO publications. Detailed information on each step is available to WHO \nstaff on the WHO intranet (3). A brief overview is provided here.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the guideline production process, which follows the same steps as other WHO publications, with details on the staff intranet and a brief overview given here.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-228",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Writing",
      "pageStart": 171,
      "pageEnd": 172,
      "target": "../sections/sec-228-1231-writing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Identify a writer early in the process and make sure this person is engaged \nthroughout the guideline’s development. The writer can be a WHO staff \nmember or contracted on a freelance basis. In either case, it is important \nto accurately estimate the demands that will be made on the person’s time. \nOnce you have an idea of the approximate length of your document, you can \nroughly calculate the time needed and can begin negotiations with an external writer if necessary. WHO does not have a standard pay scale for writing, \nbut WHO Press usually advises a minimum of US$ 0.50 per word for writers,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises identifying a writer early, whether staff or freelance, estimating time and fees, with guidance on rates and a week for 5000 words. Recommends avoiding multiple authors, since unpaid chapter drafts tend to create more work.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-229",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Legal advice on proprietary products",
      "pageStart": 172,
      "pageEnd": 172,
      "target": "../sections/sec-229-1232-legal-advice-on-proprietary-products.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO guidelines should avoid specifying proprietary products when at all \npossible. Devices and diagnostics used in interventions should be described \ngenerically to avoid identification of specific products and trademarks. If in \ndoubt, please contact WHO’s legal counsel.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises that guidelines should avoid naming proprietary products, describing devices and diagnostics generically and consulting WHO legal counsel if in doubt.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-230",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Editing and proofreading",
      "pageStart": 172,
      "pageEnd": 172,
      "target": "../sections/sec-230-1233-editing-and-proofreading.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "You will also need an editor and a proofreader. WHO press maintains lists of \napproved freelance technical editors, copy-editors and proofreaders and provides \nsample terms of reference and standard rates of pay for these tasks on the intranet. \nThe best editors and proofreaders are often booked up many months in advance, so \nplan production schedules as early as possible and reserve their time accordingly.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises engaging an editor and proofreader from lists kept by the publishing office, and booking them early because the best are reserved months ahead.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-231",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Executive clearance and approval by the Guideline Review Committee",
      "pageStart": 172,
      "pageEnd": 172,
      "target": "../sections/sec-231-1234-executive-clearance-and-approval-by-the-gui.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Committee\nGRC review of final guideline documents occurs as part of the final executive \nclearance. In headquarters, submission to the GRC is done after approval by \nthe relevant director and before submission to the assistant director-general.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Says the Guideline Review Committee reviews final guideline documents as part of executive clearance, after the relevant director's approval and before the assistant director-general.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241548960-eng/blocks/prose-sec-232",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
