      "title": "Recommendations that may be out of date",
      "pageStart": 176,
      "pageEnd": 176,
      "target": "../sections/sec-244-1254-recommendations-that-may-be-out-of-date.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Occasionally guideline developers may want to update guidelines before the \n“review-by” date, particularly if new evidence is published. This new evidence \nshould always be seen in the context of the total body of evidence supporting \nthe recommendations and thus should be part of a new or updated systematic review. Any update that involves changing recommendations needs to be \nreviewed by the GRC. Updates that add new evidence without changing the recommendations do not require review, although under certain circumstances, if \nthe topic or new evidence is highly controversial, GRC review may",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that early updates should be based on the total body of evidence through a new or updated systematic review, and that changing recommendations requires committee review while adding evidence alone does not. Says stakeholders should be told when recommendations may be invalid and updates are planned.",
